    {"key":"jim-beam","group":"bourbon","family":"American","producer":"Jim Beam","madeBy":"","style":"bourbon","chapter":"bourbon","shelf":"","library":"Jim Beam","say":"","place":"Clermont, Kentucky","year":"","yearIs":"","bottle":{"name":"Jim Beam Original Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey (White Label)","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey","mash":"Corn, rye and malted barley, with corn the larger part as the law requires; the house publishes no percentages.","still":"Sour mashed and distilled at Clermont, by the house's account first to low wine at about 125 proof, then through a doubler to high wine below 160 proof.","wood":"Four years in new, charred American white oak barrels with a number four char, by the house's account. The house gives the age as four years.","strength":"40% ABV (80 proof)","taste":"Oak and vanilla on the nose over a little spice, then caramel and vanilla in a medium body, and oak with a hint of spice on the finish.","pour":"Neat, over ice or in a highball with ginger ale or soda; it is a sound base for a Whiskey Sour or an Old Fashioned.","label":[{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Bourbon made in Kentucky and kept at least two years in new charred oak."},{"term":"80 Proof","means":"40 per cent alcohol by volume, the floor the law sets for bourbon."}]},"table":"Jim Beam White Label is the Kentucky straight bourbon from Clermont, four years in new charred oak and bottled at 80 proof. Expect vanilla, caramel and oak with a little spice: a plain bourbon to taste the others against, and an easy highball.","story":"As the brand tells it, the Beam family sold its first whiskey in Kentucky in 1795, a year that is the family's claim for its line rather than for this label, and its bourbon was sold for decades under the name Old Tub. James B. Beam (1864 to 1947) took charge of the family distillery in the 1890s, and Prohibition closed it in 1920. After repeal he rebuilt at Clermont with friends and family, and the first batch was ready in 1935. The rights to Old Tub had been lost, and in 1943 the bourbon took his own name, Jim Beam. The family tells of him carrying a jug of the house yeast home at weekends to keep it safe, and the house says it has used that strain since repeal. In 2014 Suntory of Japan bought Beam Inc., the company that then made it. At the bar the White Label is the baseline: learn its vanilla, caramel and gentle spice, and every other bourbon on the back bar is tasted against it.","others":[{"name":"Jim Beam Double Oak","note":"Aged twice, the second time in a fresh charred oak barrel, by the house's account."}],"traps":["White Label is often taken for a bourbon with no age: by the house's account it is four years old, twice the straight bourbon minimum.","The year 1795 is the Beam family's claim for an ancestor, not the age of the Jim Beam name, which the bourbon took in 1943.","Clermont rebuilt after Prohibition: the first batch there was ready in 1935."],"cards":[{"id":"age","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 jim-beam/age","prompt":"How long is Jim Beam White Label aged?","answer":"Four years in new charred oak by the house's account, twice the straight bourbon minimum."},{"id":"old-tub","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 jim-beam/old-tub","prompt":"Under what name was the Beam family's bourbon sold before it became Jim Beam?","answer":"Old Tub; the rights were lost over Prohibition, and in 1943 the bourbon took the name of James B. Beam."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 jim-beam/age","q":"A guest says Jim Beam White Label has no age. What age does the bourbon carry?","options":["Two years","Four years","Six years","Eight years"],"a":1,"explain":"The house ages it four years by its own account; two years is only the minimum for the word straight."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 jim-beam/old-tub","q":"In which year did the Beam family's bourbon take the name Jim Beam?","options":["1795","1920","1935","1943"],"a":3,"explain":"The name came in 1943, after the Old Tub rights were lost; 1935 is the year the first batch was ready at the rebuilt Clermont distillery."}],"sources":[{"title":"Jim Beam: Original Kentucky Straight Bourbon","url":"https://www.jimbeam.com/en-us/bourbons/jim-beam","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Jim Beam: How bourbon is made","url":"https://www.jimbeam.com/en-us/our-story/bourbon-process","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Jim Beam: The family history","url":"https://www.jimbeam.com/en-us/our-story/beam-history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Jim Beam","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jim_Beam","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Difford's Guide: James B. Beam Distilling Co., history","url":"https://www.diffordsguide.com/producers/42/james-b-beam-distilling-co-jim-beam/history","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"buffalo-trace","group":"bourbon","family":"American","producer":"Buffalo Trace","madeBy":"","style":"bourbon","chapter":"bourbon","shelf":"","library":"Buffalo Trace","say":"","place":"Frankfort, Franklin County, Kentucky","year":"1999","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"Buffalo Trace Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey","mash":"The distillery's Mash Bill No. 1, corn with rye and malted barley; the distillery does not publish the proportions.","still":"Distilled at the Buffalo Trace Distillery on the Kentucky River at Frankfort.","wood":"New charred oak barrels, aged in the distillery's old warehouses at Frankfort. No age statement on the label.","strength":"45% ABV (90 proof), 40% in some markets","taste":"Vanilla, mint and molasses on the nose, then brown sugar and spice giving way to toffee, dark fruit, anise and oak, with a long finish.","pour":"Neat or over one large cube; it is a natural Old Fashioned bourbon and works in a Manhattan or a Boulevardier.","label":[{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Bourbon made in Kentucky and kept at least two years in new charred oak."},{"term":"Buffalo Trace","means":"The name of the distillery and of this, its own flagship bourbon."}]},"table":"Buffalo Trace is the flagship bourbon of the Frankfort distillery of the same name, the house behind Weller and Blanton's. Its Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey, at 90 proof, brings vanilla, brown sugar and toffee with a little mint, and it makes an easy Old Fashioned.","story":"The distillery on the Kentucky River at Frankfort takes its name, by its own account, from the ancient traces that migrating buffalo wore through the wilderness to a crossing of the river there. A distillery called O.F.C. stood on the site by 1870. In 1878 it was bought by a St. Louis whiskey merchant, and from 1904 it carried his name as the George T. Stagg Distillery. Through Prohibition it kept working under a special government licence to bottle whiskey for medicinal use, one of a handful so permitted, and after repeal a metal-clad warehouse rose there that later housed Blanton's, first released in 1984 as a single barrel bourbon. The Sazerac Company bought the distillery in 1992, and in 1999 renamed it Buffalo Trace; a bourbon of the same name followed that year, made to its Mash Bill No. 1. Taste it first: it is the everyday bottle of the distillery behind Weller, Blanton's and the Antique Collection, and the rest of the family makes sense after it.","others":[{"name":"Eagle Rare 10 Year","note":"A ten year old Kentucky straight bourbon made at the same distillery."},{"name":"Blanton's Single Barrel","note":"The distillery's single barrel bourbon, first released in 1984."},{"name":"W. L. Weller Special Reserve","note":"The distillery's wheated bourbon, with wheat where this bottle has rye."}],"traps":["Buffalo Trace is both the distillery and its flagship bourbon; Weller, Blanton's and Eagle Rare come from the same Frankfort stills under their own names.","The bourbon carries no age statement, so no number of years should be quoted to a guest.","The Buffalo Trace name dates only from 1999, though whiskey has been made on the site since the nineteenth century."],"cards":[{"id":"mash-bill","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 buffalo-trace/mash-bill","prompt":"Which recipe of the distillery goes into Buffalo Trace Kentucky Straight Bourbon?","answer":"Mash Bill No. 1, corn with rye and malted barley, in proportions the distillery does not publish."},{"id":"rename","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 buffalo-trace/rename","prompt":"Under what name did the Buffalo Trace distillery work before 1999?","answer":"The George T. Stagg Distillery, its name from 1904; the Sazerac Company renamed it Buffalo Trace in 1999."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 buffalo-trace/mash-bill","q":"A guest asks how strong Buffalo Trace Kentucky Straight Bourbon is in the United States. What does the label state?","options":["90 proof","80 proof","101 proof","86 proof"],"a":0,"explain":"It is bottled at 45% ABV, 90 proof, in the United States; 80 proof is the strength of Jim Beam White Label and some export bottlings."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 buffalo-trace/rename","q":"In which year did the Frankfort distillery take the name Buffalo Trace?","options":["1870","1904","1999","1992"],"a":2,"explain":"The Sazerac Company renamed it in 1999; it had bought the distillery in 1992, and 1904 is the year it took the Stagg name."}],"sources":[{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: Buffalo Trace Bourbon","url":"https://www.buffalotracedistillery.com/our-brands/buffalo-trace/buffalo-trace-bourbon/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: Our history","url":"https://www.buffalotracedistillery.com/buffalo-trace-history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: Our distillery","url":"https://www.buffalotracedistillery.com/our-distillery/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Buffalo Trace Distillery","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buffalo_Trace_Distillery","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"VinePair: Buffalo Trace bourbon guide","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/buffalo-trace-bourbon-whiskey-guide/","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"wild-turkey","group":"bourbon","family":"American","producer":"Wild Turkey","madeBy":"","style":"bourbon","chapter":"bourbon","shelf":"","library":"Wild Turkey","say":"","place":"Lawrenceburg, Kentucky","year":"","yearIs":"","bottle":{"name":"Wild Turkey 101 Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey","mash":"Corn, rye and malted barley, with corn the larger part as the law requires; the house publishes no percentages.","still":"Distilled at the Wild Turkey Distillery in Lawrenceburg on a continuous column still, then through a doubler, with the house's own yeast strain.","wood":"New American white oak with the deep number four char, the alligator char, filled at a low proof by the house's account. No age is given here: the house's own pages differ.","strength":"50.5% ABV (101 proof)","taste":"Vanilla, toffee and orange on the nose, then a little sweetness with vanilla and caramel, oak and baking spice in the middle, and a finish of spice and orange peel.","pour":"Neat with a few drops of water, over one large cube, or shaken: its strength holds up in a Whiskey Sour, a Manhattan or an Old Fashioned.","label":[{"term":"101","means":"The proof: 50.5 per cent alcohol by volume, the bottling's one strength."},{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Bourbon made in Kentucky and kept at least two years in new charred oak."}]},"table":"Bottled at 101 proof from barrels with a deep alligator char, Wild Turkey 101 is the Kentucky straight bourbon of Lawrenceburg. Vanilla, toffee and orange peel over baking spice: the bourbon to reach for when a drink must stand up to citrus or vermouth.","story":"Wild Turkey began as a merchant's label rather than a distillery. Austin, Nichols, a New York firm of merchants, bought its bourbon from Kentucky distilleries, the Ripy family's at Lawrenceburg among them. The name comes, the story goes, from a turkey hunt in 1940, when an executive of the firm took warehouse samples along, and his friends asked a year later for more of that wild turkey bourbon; some accounts put the hunt a few years earlier. By most accounts Austin, Nichols was bottling it under that name by 1942. In 1971 the firm bought the Lawrenceburg distillery, known then as the Boulevard Distillery, and renamed it for the label. Pernod Ricard bought the distillery and the brand in 1980, and the Campari Group bought both in 2009, bringing a new distillery on the site into use in 2011. A father and son, both master distillers there across decades, shaped its style. At 101 proof, from barrels filled at a low proof so that less water goes in at bottling, it is the bar's bourbon for a drink that has to hold its own.","others":[{"name":"Wild Turkey 101 Rye","note":"A rye whiskey of the house, bottled at the same 101 proof."}],"traps":["The 101 on the label is the proof, 50.5 per cent alcohol, not an age or a batch number.","Wild Turkey 101 is not the plain Wild Turkey Bourbon: that bottling is lower in strength, at 81 proof.","The turkey hunt of 1940 is the brand's story, and accounts differ on its year: tell it as the story goes."],"cards":[{"id":"char","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 wild-turkey/char","prompt":"Which char does the house give the barrels of Wild Turkey 101?","answer":"The deep number four char, the alligator char, by the house's account, in new American white oak."},{"id":"turkey-hunt","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 wild-turkey/turkey-hunt","prompt":"Where does the name Wild Turkey come from, as the story goes?","answer":"A turkey hunt in 1940, when an Austin, Nichols executive shared warehouse samples with friends who asked for more of that wild turkey bourbon."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 wild-turkey/char","q":"A guest asks what the 101 on the Wild Turkey label means. What is it?","options":["The age in months","The batch number","The proof","The barrel count"],"a":2,"explain":"It is the proof, 50.5 per cent alcohol by volume, the one strength of the bottling; it is not a batch number, and no age is printed in its place."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 wild-turkey/turkey-hunt","q":"Which New York firm put the Wild Turkey name on its bourbon after a hunting trip?","options":["Pernod Ricard","Austin, Nichols","Campari Group","Boulevard Distillery"],"a":1,"explain":"Austin, Nichols, a firm of merchants, named and bottled it; Pernod Ricard bought the distillery and brand only in 1980."}],"sources":[{"title":"Wild Turkey: Wild Turkey 101","url":"https://www.wildturkeybourbon.com/en-us/products/wild-turkey-101/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wild Turkey: Our craft, how the bourbon is made","url":"https://www.wildturkeybourbon.com/en-us/our-craft/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Wild Turkey bourbon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wild_Turkey_(bourbon)","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Difford's Guide: Austin Nichols Wild Turkey Distillery, history","url":"https://www.diffordsguide.com/producers/678/austin-nichols-wild-turkey-distillery/history","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: How Wild Turkey got its name","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/whiskey-articles/how-wild-turkey-got-its-unusual-name/","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"woodford-reserve","group":"bourbon","family":"American","producer":"Woodford Reserve","madeBy":"Brown-Forman","style":"bourbon","chapter":"bourbon","shelf":"","library":"","say":"","place":"Versailles, Woodford County, Kentucky","year":"1996","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"Woodford Reserve Distiller's Select Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey","mash":"By the house's account 72 per cent corn, 18 per cent rye and 10 per cent malted barley.","still":"A marriage of two whiskeys: one distilled three times in copper pot stills at the Woodford Reserve Distillery near Versailles, the other made on a column still at Brown-Forman's distillery at Shively, beside Louisville.","wood":"New charred oak barrels. No age statement on the label.","strength":"45.2% ABV (90.4 proof)","taste":"Dried fruit, mint and orange under a dusting of cocoa, with faint vanilla and tobacco spice, then citrus, cinnamon, toffee and chocolate, and a long, warm finish.","pour":"Neat or on the rocks; it suits a Manhattan, an Old Fashioned and the Mint Julep.","label":[{"term":"Distiller's Select","means":"The name of the house's standard bourbon; the words have no legal meaning."},{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Bourbon made in Kentucky and kept at least two years in new charred oak."}]},"table":"Woodford Reserve Distiller's Select, a Kentucky straight bourbon from the old stone distillery near Versailles, marries copper pot still whiskey with column still whiskey and is bottled at 90.4 proof. Dried fruit, orange and cocoa with a touch of cinnamon make it a natural in a Manhattan.","story":"A distillery stood on Glenn's Creek near Versailles by about 1812, built by the Pepper family, and whiskey was made there through much of the century. In 1878 the Peppers sold the property to the partnership of Labrot & Graham, whose name it took. Brown-Forman bought the distillery in 1941 and ran it until 1968, when it closed, and the land went back to farming. In the early 1990s Brown-Forman bought it back, restored the stone buildings and put in copper pot stills, and in 1996 it released Woodford Reserve, its bourbon from the old site. The distillery itself took the Woodford Reserve name only in 2003. Distiller's Select marries their triple distilled whiskey with column still whiskey from Brown-Forman's Shively distillery. In the glass it shows that bourbon need not come from a column still alone, and that one bottle can marry the work of two kinds of still.","others":[{"name":"Woodford Reserve Rye","note":"A rye whiskey made at the same distillery near Versailles."},{"name":"Woodford Reserve Wheat Whiskey","note":"A straight wheat whiskey of the house, with wheat the larger part of the mash."}],"traps":["Distiller's Select is not pot still whiskey alone: it marries pot still whiskey from Versailles with column still whiskey from Shively.","The distillery was called Labrot & Graham until 2003; Woodford Reserve was first the name of the bourbon, from 1996.","Its strength is 90.4 proof, not 90: the label states 45.2 per cent."],"cards":[{"id":"two-stills","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 woodford-reserve/two-stills","prompt":"Which two kinds of still make the whiskey in Woodford Reserve Distiller's Select?","answer":"Copper pot stills at Versailles, run three times, and a column still at Brown-Forman's Shively distillery, married in the one bourbon."},{"id":"labrot-graham","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 woodford-reserve/labrot-graham","prompt":"What was the Woodford Reserve distillery called before 2003?","answer":"Labrot & Graham, after the partners who bought it from the Pepper family in 1878."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 woodford-reserve/two-stills","q":"A guest asks how Woodford Reserve Distiller's Select is distilled. What is the right answer?","options":["Column still alone","Pot still alone","Hybrid still alone","Pot and column still"],"a":3,"explain":"It marries triple pot still whiskey from Versailles with column still whiskey from Shively; pot still alone is the common mistake."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 woodford-reserve/labrot-graham","q":"In which year did Brown-Forman release Woodford Reserve from the restored Versailles distillery?","options":["1996","1941","2003","1968"],"a":0,"explain":"The bourbon came out in 1996; 2003 is the year the distillery itself took the Woodford Reserve name, and 1941 the year Brown-Forman first bought it."}],"sources":[{"title":"Woodford Reserve: Distiller's Select","url":"https://shop.woodfordreserve.com/distillers-select/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Woodford Reserve: Bourbon whiskey","url":"https://www.woodfordreserve.com/whiskey/straight-bourbon-whiskey/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Woodford Reserve","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodford_Reserve","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Kentucky Historical Society: Labrot & Graham Distillery marker","url":"https://history.ky.gov/markers/labrot-graham-distillery-2","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Difford's Guide: Woodford Reserve Distillery","url":"https://www.diffordsguide.com/producers/262/woodford-reserve-distillery-labrot-and-graham","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"old-forester","group":"bourbon","family":"American","producer":"Old Forester","madeBy":"Brown-Forman","style":"bourbon","chapter":"bourbon","shelf":"","library":"Old Forester","say":"","place":"Louisville, Jefferson County, Kentucky","year":"1870","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"Old Forester 86 Proof Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whisky","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whisky, spelt on the label without the e","mash":"By the brand's account its traditional recipe, 72 per cent corn, 18 per cent rye and 10 per cent malted barley.","still":"Distilled at Brown-Forman's distillery at Shively, beside Louisville, and from 2018 also at the brand's own distillery on Whiskey Row.","wood":"New charred American oak barrels. No age statement on the label.","strength":"43% ABV (86 proof)","taste":"Citrus, tropical fruit and soft florals on the nose with a pound cake sweetness, then citrus zest, ripe pear, fresh mint and clove, and a finish of spearmint and toasted oak.","pour":"Neat, over ice or in a highball; it was made lighter for mixed drinks and is a sound house bourbon for an Old Fashioned or a Mint Julep.","label":[{"term":"86 Proof","means":"43 per cent alcohol, the strength brought in for this bottling in 1959."},{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whisky","means":"Straight bourbon made in Kentucky; the brand spells whisky without the e."}]},"table":"Old Forester 86 Proof is the Louisville bourbon George Garvin Brown first sold in sealed bottles in 1870, the brand Brown-Forman was built on. Light and crisp at 86 proof, with orange, pear, mint and clove: a bourbon made for mixing.","story":"George Garvin Brown (1846 to 1917), a young pharmaceutical salesman in Louisville, began selling Old Forester in 1870 with his half-brother. He sold it only in sealed glass bottles, as a guarantee against the watering and tampering of the middlemen who then sold whiskey from the barrel, and the brand calls it America's first bottled bourbon. In 1890 he went into partnership with his accountant and friend, and the firm took the name Brown-Forman. Through Prohibition the company held a licence to bottle whiskey for medicinal use, and by its own account Old Forester is the one bourbon sold by the same company before, during and after Prohibition. The first Old Forester went out at 90 proof; in 1959 the 86 Proof came in as a lighter bourbon for the cocktails of the day, beside the 100 proof that was kept. In 2018 the brand opened a distillery on West Main Street, on the Whiskey Row of its beginnings. Pour the 86 Proof and the whole story of the sealed bottle comes with it, in a bourbon light enough to mix.","others":[{"name":"Old Forester 100 Proof","note":"The same bourbon at a higher strength, kept when the 86 Proof came in."},{"name":"Old Forester 1924 10 Year Old","note":"A ten year old bourbon of the brand's Whiskey Row Series, made to a different mash bill."},{"name":"Old Forester Rye","note":"A rye whisky of the brand at 100 proof."}],"traps":["The label spells whisky without the e, though it is a Kentucky bourbon.","The 86 Proof dates from 1959, not 1870: the first Old Forester went out at 90 proof.","First bottled bourbon is the brand's own claim: what is sure is that it was sold only in sealed glass bottles from 1870."],"cards":[{"id":"proof-1959","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 old-forester/proof-1959","prompt":"When was Old Forester 86 Proof introduced, and why?","answer":"In 1959, as a lighter bourbon for the cocktails of the day; the 100 proof was kept beside it."},{"id":"sealed-bottle","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 old-forester/sealed-bottle","prompt":"What did George Garvin Brown do differently when he began selling Old Forester in 1870?","answer":"He sold it only in sealed glass bottles, a guarantee against watering and tampering by middlemen."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 old-forester/proof-1959","q":"A guest asks how strong Old Forester 86 Proof is by volume. What is the answer?","options":["40 per cent","43 per cent","45 per cent","50 per cent"],"a":1,"explain":"Eighty-six proof is 43 per cent alcohol by volume; 45 per cent would be 90 proof, the strength of the first Old Forester."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 old-forester/sealed-bottle","q":"In 1870, how did George Garvin Brown first sell Old Forester?","options":["From the barrel","In stoneware jugs","In sealed glass bottles","By the barrel to taverns"],"a":2,"explain":"He sold it only in sealed glass bottles to guard against tampering; whiskey drawn from the barrel was exactly what he set out to replace."}],"sources":[{"title":"Old Forester: 86 Proof","url":"https://www.oldforester.com/products/old-forester-86-proof/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Old Forester Distilling Co.: history fact sheet","url":"https://www.oldforester.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/FactSheet_OFD_History.docx-2.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Old Forester: the 1924 release and the traditional recipe","url":"https://www.oldforester.com/old-forester-debuts-1924/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Kentucky Historical Society: Brown-Forman Established marker","url":"https://explorekyhistory.ky.gov/items/show/419","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Old Forester","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Forester","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"makers-mark","group":"wheated-bourbon","family":"American","producer":"Maker's Mark","madeBy":"","style":"wheated-bourbon","chapter":"bourbon","shelf":"","library":"Maker\u2019s Mark","say":"","place":"Loretto, Marion County, Kentucky","year":"1953","yearIs":"founded","bottle":{"name":"Maker's Mark Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whisky","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whisky, spelt on the label without the e","mash":"By the distillery's own account 70 per cent corn, 16 per cent soft red winter wheat and 14 per cent malted barley: wheat stands where most bourbons use rye.","still":"Fermented and distilled twice at the distillery in Loretto: first in a copper column still, then in a copper doubler.","wood":"New charred American white oak barrels, moved by hand from the upper floors of the warehouses to the cooler lower ones as they age. The label carries no age statement.","strength":"45% ABV (90 proof)","taste":"Sweet oak, vanilla and a little fruit on the nose, then caramel, cherry and soft spice, round rather than peppery, with a soft, clean finish.","pour":"Neat, with a few drops of water or over one large cube. It makes a gentle Old Fashioned and stands in a Whiskey Sour, a Gold Rush and a Mint Julep.","label":[{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whisky","means":"Straight bourbon made in Kentucky; the house spells whisky without the e."},{"term":"45% Alc./Vol.","means":"Ninety proof, the one strength of the standard bottling."}]},"table":"Maker's Mark is the wheated bourbon from Loretto, Kentucky, made with soft red winter wheat where most bourbons use rye. The bottle with the red wax is its Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whisky at 90 proof: caramel, vanilla and cherry, soft enough for a gentle Old Fashioned.","story":"Bill Samuels Sr. (died 1992) bought the old Burks' Distillery at Loretto in the autumn of 1953, and distilling began there the following year. He built his recipe around soft red winter wheat in place of rye, and the first bottles went on sale in 1958. His wife, Margie Samuels (died 1985), is credited with the name, the label, the shape of the bottle and the red wax that seals each neck, an idea said to be drawn from her collection of old Cognac bottles. In 1980 the distillery, listed as Burks' Distillery, was made a National Historic Landmark. The family sold the business to Hiram Walker & Sons in 1981 and went on managing it. The brand passed to Fortune Brands in 2005, and in 2014 Beam, the company that then held it, was sold to Suntory of Japan. Through every change the recipe and the single strength stayed as they were. For a bartender the bottle is the plain lesson in wheat: one mash, one strength, and the wheated bourbon that nearly every bar carries.","others":[{"name":"Maker's Mark 46","note":"The same bourbon given a further finish with French oak staves."},{"name":"Maker's Mark Cask Strength","note":"The same whisky bottled uncut, its strength changing from batch to batch."}],"traps":["The label spells it whisky, without the e, though it is a Kentucky bourbon and not Scotch.","No age is printed on its label, so give a guest no number of years as fact.","The wheat stands in place of rye, not of corn: it is a bourbon of at least 51 per cent corn like any other."],"cards":[{"id":"mash","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 makers-mark/mash","prompt":"What is the Maker's Mark mash bill, as the distillery gives it?","answer":"70 per cent corn, 16 per cent soft red winter wheat and 14 per cent malted barley: wheat in place of rye."},{"id":"wax","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 makers-mark/wax","prompt":"Who is credited with the name, the label and the red wax of Maker's Mark?","answer":"Margie Samuels, wife of the founder, Bill Samuels Sr.; the first bottles went on sale in 1958."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 makers-mark/mash","q":"A guest asks why Maker's Mark tastes softer than most bourbons. Which grain does it use in place of rye?","options":["Soft red winter wheat","Malted winter rye","Rolled oats","White dent corn"],"a":0,"explain":"Its recipe puts soft red winter wheat where most bourbons use rye; corn is the larger part of the mash, as the rule for bourbon requires."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 makers-mark/wax","q":"In which year did the first bottles of Maker's Mark go on sale?","options":["1953","1958","1980","1981"],"a":1,"explain":"The distillery was bought in 1953 and the first bottles followed in 1958; 1981 is the year the family sold the business."}],"sources":[{"title":"Maker's Mark: How it is made","url":"https://www.makersmark.com/en-us/distillery/how-its-made","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Maker's Mark: Kentucky Straight Bourbon","url":"https://www.makersmark.com/en-us/bourbons/makers-mark","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Maker's Mark","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maker%27s_Mark","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Difford's Guide: Maker's Mark Distillery, history","url":"https://www.diffordsguide.com/producers/126/makers-mark-distillery/history","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Kentucky Distillers' Association: an honour for the founder's son","url":"https://kybourbon.com/?p=2528","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"weller","group":"wheated-bourbon","family":"American","producer":"W. L. Weller","madeBy":"Buffalo Trace Distillery","style":"wheated-bourbon","chapter":"bourbon","shelf":"weller-special-reserve","library":"","say":"","place":"Frankfort, Franklin County, Kentucky","year":"1849","yearIs":"founded","bottle":{"name":"Weller Special Reserve","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey","mash":"A wheated bourbon mash: corn, wheat in place of rye, and malted barley. The distillery does not publish the proportions.","still":"Distilled at Buffalo Trace Distillery in Frankfort; the same wheated recipe goes into the Van Winkle bourbons.","wood":"New charred American oak barrels. No age statement: for a straight bourbon without one, that means at least four years.","strength":"45% ABV (90 proof)","taste":"Caramel and honey on a sweet nose, then butterscotch, a little fresh bread from the wheat and soft oak, with a short, sweet finish and none of the pepper of rye.","pour":"Neat, or on the rocks with one large cube. It mixes a mellow Old Fashioned, and it suits a Gold Rush or a Whiskey Sour.","label":[{"term":"The Original Wheated Bourbon","means":"The label's own claim for the Weller name: wheat in place of rye in the mash."},{"term":"Special Reserve","means":"The name of this bottling, the first step of the range; the words have no legal meaning."},{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Bourbon made in Kentucky and kept at least two years in new charred oak."}]},"table":"Made at Buffalo Trace in Frankfort, W. L. Weller is a line of wheated bourbons, and Weller Special Reserve is its green label at 90 proof. With wheat in the place of rye it pours honey, caramel and butterscotch, a soft bourbon for the guest who finds rye too peppery.","story":"The label carries the name of William Larue Weller (1825 to 1899), who went into the whiskey trade in Louisville in 1849 with his brother, as W. L. Weller & Brothers. The brand credits him with putting wheat in the mash where others used rye, though historians have long debated whether he distilled at all. In 1893 the firm hired a young salesman, Julian \"Pappy\" Van Winkle (1874 to 1965), who bought it with a fellow salesman in 1908 and later joined it to the A. Ph. Stitzel distillery. The Stitzel-Weller Distillery opened near Louisville on Derby Day in 1935 and made the wheated recipe its own. The Van Winkle family sold the distillery to Norton Simon in 1972, the stills there went quiet in the early 1990s, and in 1999 the Weller label was bought by the Sazerac Company, which moved it to Buffalo Trace in Frankfort. Special Reserve is the first step of the range that followed, and the bottle a guest who asks for a wheater is most often poured.","others":[{"name":"Weller Antique 107","note":"The same wheated recipe bottled at 107 proof."},{"name":"Weller Aged 12 Years","note":"The same recipe rested twelve years in new charred oak, deeper and oakier; the black label."},{"name":"William Larue Weller","note":"The uncut, unfiltered wheated bourbon of the Buffalo Trace Antique Collection, a yearly release."}],"traps":["Weller is made at Buffalo Trace: there is no Weller distillery, in Frankfort or anywhere else.","The year 1849 is when the Weller firm began trading, not proof that the wheated recipe is that old.","Special Reserve is the green label at 90 proof; Antique 107 and the 12 year old share its recipe, not its bottle."],"cards":[{"id":"wheat","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 weller/wheat","prompt":"What does wheated mean on Weller Special Reserve, and is the mash bill published?","answer":"Wheat takes the place of rye beside corn and malted barley; the distillery does not publish the proportions."},{"id":"name","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 weller/name","prompt":"Who was the W. L. Weller of the label, and when did his firm begin?","answer":"William Larue Weller (1825 to 1899), a Louisville whiskey merchant whose firm began trading in 1849."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 weller/wheat","q":"Weller Special Reserve carries no age statement. As a straight bourbon, what is the minimum time it has spent in oak?","options":["At least two years","At least six years","At least four years","At least twelve years"],"a":2,"explain":"A straight bourbon younger than four years must state its age, so no statement means at least four; two years is the floor for the word straight itself."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 weller/name","q":"In which year did the Sazerac Company buy the Weller label and move it to Buffalo Trace?","options":["1935","1972","1849","1999"],"a":3,"explain":"The label changed hands in 1999; 1935 is the year Stitzel-Weller opened and 1972 the year the Van Winkle family sold that distillery."}],"sources":[{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: W. L. Weller Special Reserve","url":"https://www.buffalotracedistillery.com/our-brands/w-l-weller/w-l-weller-special-reserve/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: William Larue Weller","url":"https://www.buffalotracedistillery.com/our-brands/w-l-weller/william-larue-weller/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Old Rip Van Winkle Distillery: Our legacy","url":"https://oldripvanwinkle.com/heritage/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Stitzel-Weller Distillery","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stitzel%E2%80%93Weller_Distillery","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"VinePair: W. L. Weller bourbon guide","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/wl-weller-bourbon-guide/","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"pappy-van-winkle","group":"wheated-bourbon","family":"American","producer":"Pappy Van Winkle","madeBy":"Buffalo Trace Distillery","style":"wheated-bourbon","chapter":"bourbon","shelf":"","library":"","say":"","place":"Frankfort, Franklin County, Kentucky","year":"1994","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"Pappy Van Winkle's Family Reserve 15 Year Old","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey","mash":"A wheated bourbon mash, the family's own recipe by its account: corn, wheat in place of rye, and malted barley. No proportions are published.","still":"Distilled at Buffalo Trace Distillery in Frankfort since 2002, to the Van Winkle family's wheated recipe; earlier bottlings drew on whiskey made at Stitzel-Weller.","wood":"Fifteen years in new, deeply charred American oak, from barrels chosen in the heart of the warehouses. The age is stated on the label.","strength":"53.5% ABV (107 proof)","taste":"Caramel corn, vanilla and toffee on the nose; a full, supple palate of caramel, leather and oak with a little fruit and peppery brown spice, and a long finish of spice and wood.","pour":"Neat in a tulip glass, with a few drops of water offered beside it. At this age and strength it is a bourbon to sip, and it is not built into a mixed drink.","label":[{"term":"Family Reserve","means":"The Van Winkle family's name for its older bottlings; the words have no legal meaning."},{"term":"15 Years Old","means":"An age statement: nothing in the bottle has spent fewer than fifteen years in oak."},{"term":"107 Proof","means":"53.5 per cent alcohol by volume, well above the 80 proof floor for bourbon."}]},"table":"Pappy Van Winkle is a wheated bourbon made at Buffalo Trace in Frankfort to the Van Winkle family's recipe from the old Stitzel-Weller distillery. The Family Reserve 15 Year Old is the one a guest means: fifteen years in oak at 107 proof, caramel, leather and spice, poured neat.","story":"Julian \"Pappy\" Van Winkle (1874 to 1965) began in 1893 as a salesman for W. L. Weller & Sons of Louisville. In 1908 he and a fellow salesman bought the firm; they later merged it with the A. Ph. Stitzel distillery, and on Derby Day in 1935 Stitzel-Weller opened, its bourbons built on wheat where others used rye. His son, Julian Van Winkle Jr. (died 1981), was pressed by stockholders to sell the distillery in 1972, and afterwards revived a label from before Prohibition, Old Rip Van Winkle, bottling whiskey from the old distillery's stocks. The founder's grandson carried the business on from 1981, bottling aged wheated bourbon, and in 1994 released a 20 year old named for his grandfather: Pappy Van Winkle's Family Reserve. In 2002 the family entered a joint venture with Buffalo Trace, which has distilled the whiskey since; the last Stitzel-Weller stocks went into bottles during the years that followed. The 15 Year Old, at 107 proof, is the bottling most guests mean when they ask for Pappy.","others":[{"name":"Pappy Van Winkle's Family Reserve 20 Year Old","note":"The bottling that carried the name first, in 1994: five more years in oak."},{"name":"Pappy Van Winkle's Family Reserve 23 Year Old","note":"Twenty-three years in oak, eight more than the 15 Year Old."},{"name":"Old Rip Van Winkle 10 Year","note":"The family's younger wheated bourbon, under the label revived after 1972."}],"traps":["Pappy is not a distillery: the whiskey has been distilled at Buffalo Trace since 2002, and earlier bottles drew on Stitzel-Weller stock.","Pappy Van Winkle and Weller share a wheated recipe and a distillery, yet the barrels, ages and strengths differ: one is not the other under another label.","The name on the label is the founder's, who died in 1965; the bottling that carries it dates only from 1994."],"cards":[{"id":"proof","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 pappy-van-winkle/proof","prompt":"At what strength is Pappy Van Winkle's Family Reserve 15 Year Old bottled?","answer":"107 proof, 53.5% ABV, after fifteen years in new charred oak."},{"id":"pappy","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 pappy-van-winkle/pappy","prompt":"Who was the Pappy of Pappy Van Winkle?","answer":"Julian Van Winkle Sr. (1874 to 1965), a Weller salesman from 1893 who helped found Stitzel-Weller, which opened in 1935."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 pappy-van-winkle/proof","q":"A guest asks how strong the Pappy Van Winkle 15 Year Old is. What does the label state?","options":["90 proof","107 proof","100 proof","Barrel proof, varying by release"],"a":1,"explain":"The 15 Year Old is bottled at 107 proof, 53.5% ABV; 90 proof is the strength of Weller Special Reserve, its neighbour from the same distillery."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 pappy-van-winkle/pappy","q":"Which distillery, opened on Derby Day in 1935, made the wheated bourbon the Van Winkle name was built on?","options":["Stitzel-Weller","Buffalo Trace","Bernheim","Burks' Distillery"],"a":0,"explain":"Stitzel-Weller opened near Louisville in 1935; Buffalo Trace has distilled the whiskey only since the joint venture of 2002."}],"sources":[{"title":"Old Rip Van Winkle Distillery: Our legacy","url":"https://oldripvanwinkle.com/heritage/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Old Rip Van Winkle Distillery: Our whiskeys","url":"https://oldripvanwinkle.com/whiskey/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: Pappy Van Winkle 15 Year","url":"https://www.buffalotracedistillery.com/our-brands/van-winkle/pappy-van-winkle-15-year/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: Pappy Van Winkle","url":"https://www.buffalotracedistillery.com/our-distillery/legendary-people/pappy-van-winkle/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: From Stitzel-Weller to Buffalo Trace, the chronology of Pappy Van Winkle","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/american-whiskey/from-stitzel-weller-to-buffalo-trace-the-chronology-of-pappy-van-winkle/","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"larceny","group":"wheated-bourbon","family":"American","producer":"Larceny","madeBy":"Heaven Hill","style":"wheated-bourbon","chapter":"bourbon","shelf":"","library":"","say":"","place":"Distilled in Louisville, aged at Bardstown, Kentucky","year":"2012","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"Larceny Small Batch Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey","mash":"By Heaven Hill's account 68 per cent corn, 20 per cent wheat and 12 per cent malted barley: wheat in place of rye, and a larger share of it, the house says, than its nearest rival uses.","still":"Distilled at Heaven Hill's Bernheim Distillery in Louisville, by the house's own account.","wood":"New charred American oak. At launch Heaven Hill described barrels of six to twelve years from the upper floors of its rickhouses, and later a six year old taste profile; no age statement on the label is confirmed.","strength":"46% ABV (92 proof)","taste":"Fresh bread and toffee with a little butterscotch on the nose, then buttery caramel and honey with a rich feel in the mouth, and a long finish that is gently sweet and savoury.","pour":"Neat, on the rocks or with a little water. It makes a soft Old Fashioned and a Whiskey Sour, and it is the wheater to offer a guest who asks for something like Weller.","label":[{"term":"Small Batch","means":"No legal meaning; Heaven Hill gave 100 barrels or fewer at launch and 200 or fewer later."},{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Bourbon made in Kentucky and kept at least two years in new charred oak."}]},"table":"Named for the Treasury agent of the Old Fitzgerald legend, Larceny is a wheated bourbon that Heaven Hill makes in Louisville. The Small Batch at 92 proof tastes of fresh bread, toffee and buttery caramel, the wheater to pour when Maker's Mark or Weller is not the answer.","story":"The name comes from a legend told about Old Fitzgerald, the wheated bourbon that was the flagship of Stitzel-Weller. John E. Fitzgerald, the story goes, was no distiller but a Treasury agent, one of the few men allowed the keys to a bonded warehouse, and he used them to draw whiskey from the barrels he liked; the lightened casks were known as Fitzgerald barrels. The Old Fitzgerald name dates from the nineteenth century, and Julian \"Pappy\" Van Winkle (1874 to 1965) made it a wheated bourbon at Stitzel-Weller after Prohibition. When that distillery fell silent in the early 1990s the bourbon moved to the new Bernheim Distillery in Louisville, and in 1999 Bernheim and the brand were sold to Heaven Hill, three years after the fire of 1996 at its own distillery in Bardstown. Heaven Hill launched Larceny in 2012 on the Fitzgerald story, with a keyhole cut into the label, and a barrel proof version followed in 2020. Its keyhole label makes the legend easy to tell to a guest who wants a wheater beyond the better known names.","others":[{"name":"Larceny Barrel Proof","note":"The same wheated recipe bottled uncut in batches, its strength changing with each."},{"name":"Old Fitzgerald Bottled-in-Bond","note":"Heaven Hill's older wheated label, the one the Larceny story is drawn from."}],"traps":["No distillery carries the Larceny name: Heaven Hill makes it, at its Bernheim Distillery in Louisville.","The tale of John E. Fitzgerald and his keys is a legend the brand tells, not settled history.","Small batch promises no number of barrels: the term has no legal meaning in the United States."],"cards":[{"id":"mash","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 larceny/mash","prompt":"What is the Larceny mash bill, as Heaven Hill gives it, and where is it distilled?","answer":"68 per cent corn, 20 per cent wheat and 12 per cent malted barley, distilled at the Bernheim Distillery in Louisville."},{"id":"namesake","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 larceny/namesake","prompt":"Who is Larceny named for, and what is the story?","answer":"John E. Fitzgerald, a Treasury agent who, the legend says, used his warehouse keys to draw whiskey from the barrels he liked."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 larceny/mash","q":"Larceny Small Batch is a wheated bourbon. According to Heaven Hill, what share of its mash is wheat?","options":["12 per cent","16 per cent","51 per cent","20 per cent"],"a":3,"explain":"Heaven Hill gives 68 per cent corn, 20 wheat and 12 malted barley; 16 per cent is the wheat share Maker's Mark gives for its own recipe."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 larceny/namesake","q":"In the legend behind Larceny, what was John E. Fitzgerald's job?","options":["A cooper who charred the barrels","A Louisville whiskey salesman","A Treasury agent with warehouse keys","A distiller at Bernheim"],"a":2,"explain":"The story makes him a Treasury agent, one of the few allowed the keys to a bonded warehouse; it does not make him a distiller."}],"sources":[{"title":"Larceny Bourbon: Small Batch","url":"https://larcenybourbon.com/small-batch","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Heaven Hill: the launch of Larceny Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","url":"https://heavenhill.com/news-and-notes/heaven-hill-distilleries-announces-the-launch-of-larceny-kentucky-straight-bourbon-whiskey/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Heaven Hill: Larceny launches new packaging","url":"https://heavenhill.com/news-and-notes/larceny-kentucky-straight-bourbon-whiskey-launches-new-packaging-seize-tonight-campaign/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Heaven Hill Distillery: Old Fitzgerald history","url":"https://heavenhilldistillery.com/oldfitzgerald-history.php","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Old Fitzgerald","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Fitzgerald","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"old-fitzgerald","group":"wheated-bourbon","family":"American","producer":"Old Fitzgerald","madeBy":"Heaven Hill","style":"wheated-bourbon","chapter":"bourbon","shelf":"","library":"","say":"","place":"Louisville, Jefferson County, Kentucky","year":"1870","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"Old Fitzgerald 7 Year Old Bottled-in-Bond Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey, bottled in bond","mash":"By Heaven Hill's account 68 per cent corn, 20 per cent wheat and 12 per cent malted barley: the wheated recipe that Larceny shares.","still":"Distilled by Heaven Hill at its Bernheim Distillery in Louisville, the home of its wheated recipe since 1999, in a single distilling season; the neck wrap names the season and the year.","wood":"Seven years in new charred American oak barrels with a level 3 char, by the house's account. The age is stated on the label.","strength":"50% ABV (100 proof)","taste":"Graham cracker and honey on the nose, then fresh bread, brown sugar and baking spice on the palate, with a cinnamon sweetness that fades into oak on the finish.","pour":"Neat, or with a few drops of water to open the spice. At 100 proof it holds its own over one large cube and builds a firm, rounded Old Fashioned or Manhattan.","label":[{"term":"Bottled-in-Bond","means":"One distiller, one distillery and one distilling season, at least four years in oak in a bonded warehouse, then bottled at 100 proof."},{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Bourbon made in Kentucky and kept at least two years in new charred oak; this one has seven."},{"term":"Your Key to Hospitality","means":"The label's own motto, written on the label and the base of the bottle; it carries no legal meaning."}]},"table":"Old Fitzgerald, once the main bourbon of the Stitzel-Weller Distillery, is a wheated bourbon Heaven Hill makes in Louisville; its 7 Year Old Bottled-in-Bond spends seven years in oak and is bottled at 100 proof. It pours honey, fresh bread and cinnamon, soft and firm enough for an Old Fashioned.","story":"By the brand's account the name first appeared in 1870, on a whiskey a Milwaukee liquor merchant sold to steamships, railways and private clubs, and only later on general sale. During Prohibition, in 1925 by Heaven Hill's account, the name was sold to W. L. Weller and Sons, the Louisville firm of Julian \"Pappy\" Van Winkle (1874 to 1965) and his partners, and when their Stitzel-Weller Distillery opened near Louisville in 1935, Old Fitzgerald became its main bourbon, made with wheat in place of rye; the plant was often called the Old Fitzgerald Distillery. The Van Winkle family sold the distillery in 1972, and the label went with it through a line of drinks companies. Distilling at Stitzel-Weller ended in the early 1990s and the recipe moved to the new Bernheim Distillery in Louisville; in 1999 Heaven Hill bought Bernheim from Diageo, and with it the Old Fitzgerald name and its barrels. In 2018 Heaven Hill began a twice yearly series of aged, bonded decanters, each edition of its own age, and in June 2025 it launched the 7 Year Old as the label's standing bottling. Behind a bar it is the wheater that carries the Stitzel-Weller name, at the strength the bond demands.","others":[{"name":"Old Fitzgerald Bottled-in-Bond Decanter Series","note":"Spring and fall editions since 2018, always bonded at 100 proof, each of its own age, in a faceted decanter."}],"traps":["Old Fitzgerald is no longer Stitzel-Weller whiskey: that distillery stopped in the early 1990s, and the 7 Year Old was distilled by Heaven Hill at Bernheim.","The 7 Year Old and the decanter series are different bottlings: the decanters change their age with each spring and fall edition.","Bottled-in-Bond promises at least four years, one season and 100 proof; the seven years is the label's own age statement on top of it."],"cards":[{"id":"bond","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 old-fitzgerald/bond","prompt":"What does Bottled-in-Bond promise on the Old Fitzgerald 7 Year Old Bottled-in-Bond?","answer":"One distiller, distillery and distilling season, at least four years in a bonded warehouse, and bottling at 100 proof."},{"id":"heaven-hill","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 old-fitzgerald/heaven-hill","prompt":"In which year did Heaven Hill buy the Old Fitzgerald name?","answer":"1999, from Diageo, together with the Bernheim Distillery in Louisville and the label's barrels."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 old-fitzgerald/bond","q":"A guest reads Bottled-in-Bond on the Old Fitzgerald 7 Year Old. At what strength must a bonded bourbon be bottled?","options":["80 proof","90 proof","107 proof","100 proof"],"a":3,"explain":"The bonded rules fix the strength at 100 proof, 50% ABV, reached with water alone; 107 proof is the Pappy Van Winkle 15 Year Old's strength, and no bonded figure."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 old-fitzgerald/heaven-hill","q":"Which distillery came to Heaven Hill with the Old Fitzgerald name in 1999?","options":["Bernheim","Stitzel-Weller","Buffalo Trace","Old Judge"],"a":0,"explain":"Heaven Hill bought the Bernheim Distillery in Louisville from Diageo in 1999, the brand with it; Stitzel-Weller had stopped distilling some years before."}],"sources":[{"title":"Heaven Hill: Old Fitzgerald unveils its 7 Year Old Bottled-in-Bond","url":"https://heavenhill.com/news-and-notes/your-key-to-hospitality-old-fitzgerald-bourbon-unveils-7-year-old-bottled-in-bond/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Heaven Hill Distillery: Old Fitzgerald history","url":"https://heavenhilldistillery.com/oldfitzgerald-history.php","kind":"primary"},{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.88, Bottled in bond","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-F/section-5.88","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Old Fitzgerald","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Fitzgerald","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: the Old Fitzgerald 7 Year Bottled-in-Bond launch","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/whiskey-new-releases/old-fitzgerald-returns-with-7-year-bottled-in-bond-signature-expression/","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"four-roses","group":"high-rye-bourbon","family":"American","producer":"Four Roses","madeBy":"","style":"high-rye-bourbon","chapter":"bourbon","shelf":"","library":"Four Roses","say":"","place":"Lawrenceburg, Anderson County, Kentucky","year":"1888","yearIs":"founded","bottle":{"name":"Four Roses Small Batch Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey","mash":"A marriage of the distillery's two published mash bills: B, 60 per cent corn, 35 per cent rye and 5 per cent malted barley, and E, 75 per cent corn, 20 per cent rye and 5 per cent malted barley.","still":"Fermented for at least three days, then distilled at Lawrenceburg on a beer column still and a pot-shaped doubler; by the distillery's account the spirit leaves the doubler at about 138 to 140 proof.","wood":"New charred oak, aged in the single-storey rack warehouses at Cox's Creek; the distillery says it uses six- and seven-year-old bourbons. The label carries no age statement.","strength":"45% ABV (90 proof)","taste":"Ripe fruit, sweet oak and caramel on the nose, then red berries and dried spice on the palate, with a soft, long finish.","pour":"Neat or on the rocks. Its rye makes a spicy Manhattan or Old Fashioned, and it works well in a Boulevardier.","label":[{"term":"Small Batch","means":"No legal meaning: here a mingling of four of the house's ten recipes, OBSK, OESK, OBSO and OESO."},{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Bourbon made in Kentucky and aged at least two years in new charred oak, with nothing added."}]},"table":"Four Roses Small Batch is a Lawrenceburg, Kentucky bourbon that marries four of the distillery's ten recipes, two of them from a mash of 35 per cent rye. At 90 proof it brings red berries, caramel and dried spice, and it stirs into a Manhattan with ease.","story":"A corsage of four red roses gave the bourbon its name, as the brand tells it: a Southern belle wore it to a ball to accept a proposal. A Louisville whiskey merchant registered Four Roses as a trademark in 1888. The distillery at Lawrenceburg, built in 1910 in the Spanish Mission style, stands on the National Register of Historic Places. Seagram bought the Frankfort Distilleries, and Four Roses with them, in 1943. In the 1950s Seagram turned the Four Roses sold at home into a blended whiskey, while straight bourbon under the name went abroad, to Europe and Japan. The single-storey warehouses at Cox's Creek were built low, so that the top and bottom racks age alike. In 2002 Kirin Brewery bought the brand, ended the blend and brought Kentucky straight bourbon back to American shelves. Small Batch followed in 2006, introduced at the Kentucky Bourbon Festival. The lesson it carries is recipe: two mash bills and five yeasts make ten bourbons, and this bottle marries four of them.","others":[{"name":"Four Roses Bourbon","note":"The house's entry bottling, once known as Yellow Label."},{"name":"Four Roses Small Batch Select","note":"Non-chill filtered and bottled at 104 proof, first introduced in 2019."}],"traps":["The yearly Limited Edition Small Batch is another bottling, uncut and drawn from older bourbons; the standard Small Batch is 90 proof.","In a recipe code such as OBSK, the B or E names the mash bill and the K or O names the yeast.","Four Roses sold in America from the 1950s until 2002 was a blend; the straight bourbon of those years went abroad."],"cards":[{"id":"recipes","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 four-roses/recipes","prompt":"Which four recipes does Four Roses marry for its Small Batch?","answer":"OBSK, OESK, OBSO and OESO: both mash bills, B and E, each with the yeasts K and O."},{"id":"kirin","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 four-roses/kirin","prompt":"In which year did Kirin buy Four Roses and bring its straight bourbon back to America?","answer":"2002. Under Seagram, from the 1950s, the Four Roses sold in America had been a blended whiskey."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 four-roses/recipes","q":"A guest asks why Four Roses Small Batch tastes spicier than many bourbons. What share of rye does its mash bill B carry?","options":["20 per cent","10 per cent","35 per cent","51 per cent"],"a":2,"explain":"Mash bill B is 60 per cent corn, 35 per cent rye and 5 per cent malted barley; 20 per cent is the rye of the other bill, E."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 four-roses/kirin","q":"In which year did Kirin buy Four Roses and end the blended whiskey sold under its name in America?","options":["2002","1943","1960","2006"],"a":0,"explain":"Kirin bought the brand in 2002; 1943 is the year Seagram bought it, and Small Batch followed in 2006."}],"sources":[{"title":"Four Roses: Small Batch","url":"https://www.fourrosesbourbon.com/bourbon/small-batch","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Four Roses: The story of Small Batch","url":"https://www.fourrosesbourbon.com/blog/the-story-of-small-batch","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Four Roses: Our process","url":"https://www.fourrosesbourbon.com/our-process","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Four Roses: Heritage","url":"https://www.fourrosesbourbon.com/heritage","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Four Roses","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four_Roses","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"old-grand-dad","group":"high-rye-bourbon","family":"American","producer":"Old Grand-Dad","madeBy":"Jim Beam","style":"high-rye-bourbon","chapter":"bourbon","shelf":"","library":"","say":"","place":"Clermont, Bullitt County, Kentucky","year":"1882","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"Old Grand-Dad Bonded Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey, bottled in bond","mash":"Beam's product pages give no figures. Its own buyer's guides and trade accounts give the high rye recipe as 63 per cent corn, 27 per cent rye and 10 per cent malted barley, the mash Basil Hayden is widely said to share.","still":"Distilled at the Jim Beam distillery in Clermont, Kentucky. Under the bond rule, all of it comes from one distillery and one distilling season.","wood":"New charred oak, at least four years in a bonded warehouse, as the bond rule requires. The label carries no age statement.","strength":"50% ABV (100 proof)","taste":"Cinnamon, citrus peel and toasted oak with a little dried fruit on the nose, then sweet vanilla and rye warmth on the palate, and a long, peppery finish.","pour":"Neat, with a little water or over one large cube. At 100 proof it keeps its shape in an Old Fashioned, a Manhattan or a Boulevardier.","label":[{"term":"Bonded","means":"Bottled in bond: one distillery, one distilling season, at least four years in a bonded warehouse and bottled at 100 proof."},{"term":"Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Bourbon aged at least two years in new charred oak, with nothing added."}]},"table":"Old Grand-Dad Bonded is a high rye bourbon made by Jim Beam in Clermont, Kentucky, bottled in bond at 100 proof. Cinnamon, citrus peel and pepper over vanilla make a spirited Old Fashioned that keeps its rye through the sugar.","story":"The label begins with Basil Hayden Sr. (died 1804), a Catholic farmer and distiller from Maryland who settled near Bardstown, Kentucky, and who, by the family's tradition, put more rye in his mash than his neighbours did. His grandson, a distiller, named a bourbon for him in 1882: Old Grand-Dad. At the end of the 1890s the brand passed to the Wathen family, whose whiskey business grew into the American Medicinal Spirits Company, and Old Grand-Dad was sold as medicinal whiskey under Prohibition, as its histories tell it. That company led to National Distillers, which made Old Grand-Dad near Frankfort. At the end of the 1980s National Distillers sold its spirits brands to Jim Beam Brands, and the whiskey came to be distilled at Beam's Clermont distillery. In 1992 the grandfather's own name went on a second label, Basil Hayden's, from the same high rye mash. For a bartender the Bonded bottle teaches two lessons at once: what rye does to a bourbon, and what the bond rule promises on a label.","others":[{"name":"Old Grand-Dad 114","note":"The same high rye bourbon bottled at 114 proof."},{"name":"Old Grand-Dad Bourbon","note":"The 80 proof bottling, made without the bond."}],"traps":["Old Grand-Dad has no distillery of its own: Jim Beam makes it at Clermont.","The standard Bonded carries no age statement: the bond promises at least four years and no more. The yearly 7 Year Bonded is another bottling.","Bonded here is the rule of the bottle, not the style: the label sits with high rye bourbon for its mash."],"cards":[{"id":"bond","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 old-grand-dad/bond","prompt":"What does the word Bonded promise on Old Grand-Dad Bonded?","answer":"One distillery and one distilling season, at least four years in a bonded warehouse, bottled at 100 proof."},{"id":"namesake","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 old-grand-dad/namesake","prompt":"Who is the grandfather that Old Grand-Dad is named for?","answer":"Basil Hayden Sr., a Maryland distiller who settled near Bardstown; his grandson named the bourbon for him in 1882."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 old-grand-dad/bond","q":"A guest asks what Bonded promises on Old Grand-Dad Bonded. How long, at least, has the whiskey aged in a bonded warehouse?","options":["Two years","Four years","Six years","Seven years"],"a":1,"explain":"The bond rule asks for at least four years, one distilling season and 100 proof; two years is the floor for any straight bourbon."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 old-grand-dad/namesake","q":"Old Grand-Dad was named in 1882 for a grandfather. Which other bourbon label carries that grandfather's name?","options":["Knob Creek","Booker's","Elijah Craig","Basil Hayden"],"a":3,"explain":"Basil Hayden, launched in 1992 from the same high rye mash, carries the grandfather's name; Knob Creek was launched beside it but names a place."}],"sources":[{"title":"Beam: About Old Grand-Dad","url":"https://www.beamdistilling.com/old-grand-dad-bourbon/about-us","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Beam: Old Grand-Dad Bottled in Bond","url":"https://www.beamdistilling.com/old-grand-dad-bourbon/whiskies/old-grand-dad-bottled-in-bond","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Old Grand-Dad","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Grand-Dad","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"VinePair: Old Grand-Dad bourbon guide","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/ntk-old-grand-dad-bourbon-guide/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Whiskey University: Basil Hayden Sr.","url":"https://www.whiskeyuniv.com/basil-hayden-sr","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"basil-hayden","group":"high-rye-bourbon","family":"American","producer":"Basil Hayden","madeBy":"Jim Beam","style":"high-rye-bourbon","chapter":"bourbon","shelf":"","library":"","say":"","place":"Clermont, Bullitt County, Kentucky","year":"1992","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"Basil Hayden Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey","mash":"Corn, a large share of rye and malted barley. Beam's product pages give no figures, and the recipe is widely said to be the high rye mash of Old Grand-Dad.","still":"Distilled by Beam at Clermont, Kentucky; the producer says no more of the stills for this bottling.","wood":"New charred American oak. The label carries no age statement.","strength":"40% ABV (80 proof)","taste":"Vanilla, brown sugar and charred oak, then a light spice of black pepper and citrus from the rye, with a little honey; light in body at its low strength.","pour":"Neat, over one large cube or in a highball. At 80 proof it makes a gentle Whiskey Sour; for a bolder Old Fashioned, Old Grand-Dad Bonded carries more.","label":[{"term":"Basil Hayden","means":"The name dropped its possessive in 2021; older bottles read Basil Hayden's."},{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Bourbon made in Kentucky and aged at least two years in new charred oak, with nothing added."}]},"table":"Light in body at 80 proof, Basil Hayden is a high rye Kentucky straight bourbon made by Beam at Clermont, with vanilla, brown sugar and a peppery rye edge. It is widely said to share its mash with Old Grand-Dad, and pours the gentler of the two.","story":"Basil Hayden Sr. (died 1804), whose name the bourbon carries, was a Catholic farmer from St. Mary's County, Maryland, who led families of his faith into the country near Bardstown, Kentucky, in the 1780s, as its histories tell it. Family tradition holds that his mash carried more rye than his neighbours' did. In 1882 his grandson named Old Grand-Dad for him, and more than a century passed before the grandfather's own name came to a label. In 1992 Beam's master distiller of the time created the Small Batch Bourbon Collection, four bourbons for slow sipping: Booker's, Baker's, Knob Creek and Basil Hayden's. The other three stood at higher strengths; Basil Hayden's was bottled at 80 proof, the floor the law allows, from a high rye recipe. In 2021 the label dropped its possessive and became Basil Hayden, with new packaging and, by the reports of the time, the same whiskey. Pour it for a guest who wants the spice of rye without the heat of a stronger bourbon.","others":[{"name":"Basil Hayden 10 Year","note":"The same label with a ten-year age statement on the bottle."}],"traps":["Basil Hayden's is the old name: the possessive was dropped in 2021, and the recipe did not change.","Its 80 proof is bourbon's legal floor for bottling, not a sign of a lighter recipe: the spice comes from the rye.","It is widely said to share its recipe with Old Grand-Dad, a mash with more rye than Jim Beam's own white label bourbon."],"cards":[{"id":"proof","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 basil-hayden/proof","prompt":"At what strength is Basil Hayden bottled?","answer":"80 proof, 40 per cent alcohol, the minimum strength the law allows for bourbon."},{"id":"collection","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 basil-hayden/collection","prompt":"Into which Beam range was Basil Hayden's launched in 1992?","answer":"The Small Batch Bourbon Collection, beside Booker's, Baker's and Knob Creek."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 basil-hayden/proof","q":"A guest wants a light, gentle bourbon and asks for Basil Hayden. At what strength is it bottled?","options":["86 proof","100 proof","Barrel proof","80 proof"],"a":3,"explain":"Basil Hayden is bottled at 80 proof, the floor for bourbon; 100 proof is the strength of its sibling, Old Grand-Dad Bonded."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 basil-hayden/collection","q":"In 1992 Basil Hayden's was launched as part of which Beam range?","options":["The Bottled in Bond series","The Small Batch Bourbon Collection","The Single Barrel Select","The Signature Craft Series"],"a":1,"explain":"It was one of four bourbons of the Small Batch Bourbon Collection, beside Booker's, Baker's and Knob Creek; the bond is the rule of Old Grand-Dad Bonded."}],"sources":[{"title":"Beam: Proof in the Small Batch Collection","url":"https://www.beamdistilling.com/articles/lowest-proof-bourbon-small-batch/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Basil Hayden's","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basil_Hayden%27s","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: Basil Hayden and Old Grand-Dad compared","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/whiskey-styles/bourbon/are-basil-hayden-and-old-grand-dad-the-same/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Tasting Table: Basil Hayden bottle guide","url":"https://www.tastingtable.com/1529734/basil-hayden-bourbon-ultimate-bottle-guide","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Whiskey University: Basil Hayden Sr.","url":"https://www.whiskeyuniv.com/basil-hayden-sr","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"bulleit","group":"high-rye-bourbon","family":"American","producer":"Bulleit","madeBy":"Diageo","style":"high-rye-bourbon","chapter":"bourbon","shelf":"","library":"","say":"BUL-it","place":"Shelbyville, Shelby County, Kentucky","year":"1987","yearIs":"founded","bottle":{"name":"Bulleit Bourbon Frontier Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey","mash":"Corn, rye and malted barley, with a high share of rye by the brand's account. The recipe is widely given as 68 per cent corn, 28 per cent rye and 4 per cent malted barley.","still":"Distilled for Diageo in Kentucky. Before 2017 it was made under contract, much of it at Four Roses in Lawrenceburg until about 2014; Diageo opened the Bulleit Distilling Co. near Shelbyville in March 2017. The label does not name the distillery behind a bottle.","wood":"New charred American oak. The label carries no age statement.","strength":"45% ABV (90 proof)","taste":"A woody nose with a hint of smoke and spice, then maple, oak and nutmeg with light toffee on the palate, and a clean, dry finish.","pour":"Over one large cube, in a highball with ginger ale or in a Whiskey Sour; its rye stands up in an Old Fashioned and a Manhattan.","label":[{"term":"Frontier Whiskey","means":"No legal meaning: the brand's name for its range, after the frontier tale it tells."},{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Bourbon made in Kentucky and aged at least two years in new charred oak, with nothing added."}]},"table":"Bulleit Bourbon is a Kentucky straight bourbon with a high share of rye, made by Diageo, which opened the Bulleit distillery near Shelbyville in 2017. At 90 proof it brings maple, oak and nutmeg and its dry, spicy edge carries through the lemon of a Whiskey Sour.","story":"Bulleit's tale begins as the brand tells it: in the 1830s an ancestor of the founder made a high rye whiskey in Kentucky, and the making stopped when he vanished around 1860 on a journey with his whiskey. In 1987 a descendant, a lawyer, founded Bulleit Frontier Whiskey on that story, with whiskey made for him under contract rather than at a distillery of his own. The brand passed through Seagram's hands and went to Diageo in the division of Seagram's drinks business, while the Four Roses distillery at Lawrenceburg went on making the bourbon under contract. That contract ran out around 2014, the year Diageo broke ground on a distillery of its own near Shelbyville, in Shelby County. The Bulleit Distilling Co. opened there in March 2017, and other Kentucky distillers supplied whiskey in the years between. Behind the bar it is the high rye bourbon of a great many rails, dry and spicy beside the corn, and a name guests often call for.","others":[{"name":"Bulleit Rye","note":"A rye whiskey, not a bourbon: ask which Bulleit a guest means."},{"name":"Bulleit 10 Year","note":"The bourbon with a ten-year age statement on the label."},{"name":"Bulleit Barrel Strength","note":"The bourbon bottled uncut, its strength varying by batch."}],"traps":["Bulleit Bourbon and Bulleit Rye are different whiskeys: a guest who asks for Bulleit should be asked which.","Bulleit is not an old distillery: the label was founded in 1987 on a family tale, and its own distillery opened in 2017.","The 1830s ancestor and his recipe are the brand's story, not a documented history."],"cards":[{"id":"mash","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 bulleit/mash","prompt":"What share of rye is widely given for the Bulleit Bourbon mash?","answer":"28 per cent rye, beside 68 per cent corn and 4 per cent malted barley."},{"id":"shelbyville","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 bulleit/shelbyville","prompt":"Where, and in which year, did Bulleit's own distillery open?","answer":"The Bulleit Distilling Co. near Shelbyville, Kentucky, opened in March 2017."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 bulleit/mash","q":"A guest asks how much rye is in Bulleit Bourbon. Which share is widely given for its mash?","options":["28 per cent","8 per cent","51 per cent","95 per cent"],"a":0,"explain":"The recipe is widely given as 68 per cent corn, 28 per cent rye and 4 per cent malted barley; 51 per cent is the minimum of corn, not of rye."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 bulleit/shelbyville","q":"In which year did the Bulleit Distilling Co. open near Shelbyville, Kentucky?","options":["1987","2001","2017","2014"],"a":2,"explain":"It opened in March 2017; ground was broken in 2014, and 1987 is the year the label was founded."}],"sources":[{"title":"Bulleit: The story","url":"https://www.bulleit.com/the-story","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bulleit: Bourbon","url":"https://www.bulleit.com/whiskeys/bulleit-bourbon","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Bulleit Bourbon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bulleit_Bourbon","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Whisky Advocate: The Bulleit distillery opens","url":"https://whiskyadvocate.com/bulleit-distillery-is-finally-open/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Whisky Advocate: The modern revival of Four Roses","url":"https://whiskyadvocate.com/The-Modern-Resurrection-of-Four-Roses","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"rabbit-hole","group":"high-rye-bourbon","family":"American","producer":"Rabbit Hole","madeBy":"","style":"high-rye-bourbon","chapter":"bourbon","shelf":"rabbit-hole-heigold","library":"Rabbit Hole","say":"","place":"Louisville, Jefferson County, Kentucky","year":"2012","yearIs":"founded","bottle":{"name":"Rabbit Hole Heigold Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey, high rye","mash":"By the distillery's account 70 per cent corn, 25 per cent malted German rye and 5 per cent malted barley: both small grains are malted, the Double Malt of its label.","still":"Distilled in Louisville on a custom copper column still built by Vendome, by the distillery's own account. Its early whiskeys were made under contract elsewhere before the distillery opened in 2018.","wood":"New American white oak barrels, toasted and then charred, filled at 110 proof. No age statement on the front label; the distillery's own pages give three or four years.","strength":"47.5% ABV (95 proof)","taste":"Toasted malt, orange peel and baked apple on the nose, then butterscotch and bright citrus on a buttery palate, and a finish that builds into black pepper and cinnamon.","pour":"Neat, or with a splash of water. Its rye and its strength carry a Boulevardier, a Manhattan or an Old Fashioned.","label":[{"term":"High Rye","means":"No legal meaning: here 25 per cent rye beside 70 per cent corn."},{"term":"Double Malt","means":"Both small grains, the rye and the barley, are malted."}]},"table":"Rabbit Hole Heigold comes from Rabbit Hole's distillery in Louisville: a high rye Kentucky straight bourbon with its rye and barley both malted, bottled at 95 proof. Orange peel and butterscotch give way to pepper and cinnamon, and it makes a lively Boulevardier.","story":"Rabbit Hole was founded in Louisville in 2012 by a psychologist turned distiller. Its first whiskeys were made to its recipes elsewhere while it built its own, and the distillery opened in the spring of 2018, with a copper column still made by Vendome, the Louisville coppersmiths. In 2019 Pernod Ricard took a majority share, and the same year the range was named for the city's history, with Heigold among the new bottles. The name is that of Christian Heigold (died 1865), a German stonecutter of Louisville. After the anti-immigrant unrest of the 1850s he carved the front of his house with busts of American figures and mottos of loyalty to the city and the Union, and he died soon after it was finished. When the house came down, the carved facade was saved; it was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1978 and moved in 2007 to River Road. Heigold teaches what malting the small grains brings to a high rye mash, and carries a Louisville story in the word on its label.","others":[{"name":"Rabbit Hole Cavehill","note":"A four grain bourbon of corn, malted wheat, malted barley and honey malted barley, at 95 proof."},{"name":"Rabbit Hole Dareringer","note":"A wheated straight bourbon finished in Pedro Xim\u00e9nez sherry casks, at 93 proof."}],"traps":["Not Cavehill, the four grain bourbon of the same house: Heigold is the high rye.","Heigold is bourbon, 70 per cent corn: a guest who asks for a rye whiskey wants another bottle.","Heigold is named for a Louisville stonecutter of the 1800s, not for the founder."],"cards":[{"id":"double-malt","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 rabbit-hole/double-malt","prompt":"What does Double Malt mean on Rabbit Hole Heigold?","answer":"Both small grains are malted: 70 per cent corn, 25 per cent malted German rye and 5 per cent malted barley."},{"id":"namesake","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 rabbit-hole/namesake","prompt":"Who is Rabbit Hole Heigold named for?","answer":"Christian Heigold, a German stonecutter of Louisville who carved patriotic busts on his house front; he died in 1865."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 rabbit-hole/double-malt","q":"A guest reads Double Malt on a bottle of Rabbit Hole Heigold. Which of its grains are malted?","options":["The corn and the rye","Only the barley","The rye and the barley","The corn and the wheat"],"a":2,"explain":"Its malted German rye and its malted barley give the Double Malt; the corn, 70 per cent of the mash, is not malted, and there is no wheat."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 rabbit-hole/namesake","q":"Rabbit Hole Heigold carries the name of a Louisville man of the 1850s. What was his trade?","options":["A cooper","A stonecutter","A riverboat pilot","A coppersmith"],"a":1,"explain":"Christian Heigold was a German stonecutter who carved the front of his house; the coppersmiths of the story are Vendome, who built the still."}],"sources":[{"title":"Rabbit Hole Distillery: Heigold","url":"https://www.rabbitholedistillery.com/products/heigold-kentucky-straight-bourbon-whiskey","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Rabbit Hole Distillery: Our craft","url":"https://www.rabbitholedistillery.com/pages/our-craft","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Whisky Advocate: Rabbit Hole and the Pernod Ricard sale","url":"https://whiskyadvocate.com/rabbit-hole-distillery-pernod-ricard-sale","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Historical Marker Database: The Heigold Facade","url":"https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=104120","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"National Park Service: National Register file, Heigold House Facade","url":"https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/78001363","kind":"primary"}]},
    {"key":"eh-taylor","group":"bottled-in-bond","family":"American","producer":"E. H. Taylor","madeBy":"Buffalo Trace Distillery","style":"bottled-in-bond","chapter":"american-labels","shelf":"","library":"","say":"","place":"Frankfort, Franklin County, Kentucky","year":"","yearIs":"","bottle":{"name":"Colonel E.H. Taylor, Jr. Small Batch Bottled in Bond Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey, bottled in bond","mash":"Buffalo Trace's Mash Bill No. 1, by the distillery's own page: corn with rye and malted barley, widely described as low in rye. No percentages are published.","still":"Distilled at the Buffalo Trace Distillery on the Kentucky River at Frankfort. Under the bond, all of it comes from one distillery and one distilling season.","wood":"New charred oak, aged at Frankfort in warehouses that, as the brand tells it, Taylor himself built. No age statement on the label: the bond asks at least four years.","strength":"50% ABV (100 proof)","taste":"Caramel corn sweetness with butterscotch and liquorice, then a soft feel in the mouth that turns to gentle spice, pepper and tobacco on the finish.","pour":"Neat or with a few drops of water. At 100 proof it builds a firm Old Fashioned and a Manhattan that the vermouth does not swamp.","label":[{"term":"Small Batch","means":"No legal meaning in the United States: a marriage of a chosen number of barrels."},{"term":"Bottled in Bond","means":"One distilling season, one distiller at one distillery, at least four years in a bonded warehouse, and exactly 100 proof."},{"term":"Colonel E.H. Taylor, Jr.","means":"The Frankfort distiller the line is named for, who fought for the bond law of 1897."}]},"table":"Colonel E. H. Taylor is the Buffalo Trace bourbon named for the Frankfort distiller who fought for the Bottled-in-Bond Act of 1897. The Small Batch Bottled in Bond is his law in a bottle, one season at 100 proof: caramel corn, butterscotch and pepper.","story":"Edmund Haynes Taylor Jr. (1830 to 1923) was a Frankfort banker, sixteen years the town's mayor, and a distiller who wanted a buyer to be able to trust a bottle of bourbon. Around 1870 he bought a small distillery at Leestown, beside Frankfort, and christened it O.F.C. When he fell into debt, George T. Stagg took the distillery over in 1878 and kept Taylor on to run it, and by Buffalo Trace's account Taylor built its Warehouse C in 1885; the site became Buffalo Trace. In 1887 Taylor built a new distillery on Glenn's Creek in Woodford County, the castle that made Old Taylor. He pressed Congress for a law that would let a distiller age whiskey in a government bonded warehouse and sell it under a federal stamp that vouched for its season, its age and its strength, and the Bottled-in-Bond Act passed in 1897. In 2009 Buffalo Trace bought the Old Taylor label and its barrels from Beam, and it later began a line of bourbons in the colonel's full name. The Small Batch is the bottle of that line a bar keeps open, and the plainest way to show a guest what the bond means: the law and the name of the man who fought for it, on one label.","others":[{"name":"Colonel E.H. Taylor, Jr. Single Barrel","note":"One barrel to a bottling, aged in Warehouse C, also bottled in bond at 100 proof with no age statement."},{"name":"E.H. Taylor, Jr. Bottled in Bond","note":"A far older bonded bourbon, more than fifteen years in oak, which joined the yearly Antique Collection in 2025."}],"traps":["No Taylor distillery makes it: the bourbon is distilled at Buffalo Trace in Frankfort, on the site of Taylor's old O.F.C.","Small Batch has no legal meaning; Bottled in Bond has, and it is the term that sets the 100 proof and the single season.","Old Taylor and Colonel E.H. Taylor are different labels, and the castle on Glenn's Creek is not where this bourbon is made."],"cards":[{"id":"bond-law","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 eh-taylor/bond-law","prompt":"Which federal law did E. H. Taylor fight for?","answer":"The Bottled-in-Bond Act, passed by Congress in 1897."},{"id":"mash-bill","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 eh-taylor/mash-bill","prompt":"Which recipe goes into Colonel E.H. Taylor Small Batch Bottled in Bond?","answer":"Buffalo Trace's Mash Bill No. 1: corn, rye and malted barley, with no percentages published."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 eh-taylor/mash-bill","q":"A guest asks which Buffalo Trace recipe goes into Colonel E.H. Taylor Small Batch Bottled in Bond. Which is it?","options":["Mash Bill No. 2","A wheated mash","Mash Bill No. 1","A four grain mash"],"a":2,"explain":"The distillery's own page names Mash Bill No. 1, corn with rye and malted barley; Mash Bill No. 2 is another of the distillery's recipes, not this one."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 eh-taylor/bond-law","q":"In which year did the Bottled-in-Bond Act that E. H. Taylor fought for pass?","options":["1897","1870","1887","1920"],"a":0,"explain":"Congress passed the act in 1897; 1887 is the year Taylor built his Old Taylor distillery on Glenn's Creek."}],"sources":[{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: E. H. Taylor, Jr. Small Batch","url":"https://www.buffalotracedistillery.com/our-brands/e-h-taylor-jr/e-h-taylor-jr-small-batch/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: Col. Edmund Haynes Taylor, Jr.","url":"https://www.buffalotracedistillery.com/our-distillery/legendary-people/edmund-haynes-taylor-jr/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Edmund H. Taylor Jr.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edmund_H._Taylor_Jr.","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Old Taylor","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Taylor","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"VinePair: the 2025 Antique Collection and the E. H. Taylor Bottled in Bond","url":"https://vinepair.com/booze-news/buffalo-trace-antique-collection-2025-eh-taylor-bottled-in-bond","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"new-riff","group":"bottled-in-bond","family":"American","producer":"New Riff","madeBy":"","style":"bottled-in-bond","chapter":"american-labels","shelf":"","library":"","say":"","place":"Newport, Campbell County, Kentucky","year":"2014","yearIs":"founded","bottle":{"name":"New Riff Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey Bottled in Bond","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey, bottled in bond","mash":"By the distillery's own account 65 per cent corn, 30 per cent rye and 5 per cent malted barley, from grain grown without genetic modification.","still":"Made at Newport to what the distillery calls the Kentucky Regimen: a sour mash, fermented in open tanks and distilled on the grain through a sixty-foot copper column still, then a doubler.","wood":"New charred American oak. The distillery gives the age as at least four years, the bond's minimum, and bottles it without chill filtration.","strength":"50% ABV (100 proof)","taste":"Butterscotch and fresh oak on the nose, then vanilla and a rye spice of clove, cinnamon and mint, with dark berries, white pepper and clove on a long finish.","pour":"Neat or with a little water. Its rye and its strength stand up in an Old Fashioned or a Boulevardier.","label":[{"term":"Bottled in Bond","means":"One distilling season at one distillery, four years or more in a bonded warehouse, bottled at exactly 100 proof."},{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Bourbon made in Kentucky and kept at least two years in new charred oak, with nothing added."}]},"table":"New Riff is the distillery at Newport, Kentucky, across the Ohio River from Cincinnati, that sold no bourbon of its own make until it could be bonded. Its Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey Bottled in Bond is 30 per cent rye at 100 proof: butterscotch, oak and spice.","story":"New Riff opened its doors in May 2014 at Newport, on the Kentucky bank of the Ohio River facing Cincinnati. Its founders, who came from a Northern Kentucky drinks shop, set out to make bourbon of the distillery's own make, and resolved that none would go on sale until it met the rule of 1897: one season, one distillery, at least four years in a bonded warehouse and exactly 100 proof. The distillery settled on a mash of 65 per cent corn, 30 rye and 5 malted barley, soured and fermented in open tanks and distilled on the grain through a tall copper column still and a doubler, the method it calls the Kentucky Regimen. Four years on, in August 2018, the first bourbon distilled at Newport went on sale at the distillery, bottled in bond and without chill filtration, and it reached other states that autumn. Single barrel bottlings of the same mash followed, and in 2024 an eight year old. For a bartender New Riff shows that the bond is not only the old houses' word: a young distillery took it up as its promise of where and when its whiskey was made, and kept it on its standard bottle.","others":[{"name":"New Riff Single Barrel","note":"The same mash, at least four years old, one barrel to a bottling at barrel strength."},{"name":"New Riff 8-Year-Old Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","note":"The same mash given at least eight years, at 100 proof without chill filtration, but not bonded: it marries two distilling seasons."}],"traps":["Newport is in Kentucky, across the river from Cincinnati in Ohio: this is a Kentucky straight bourbon.","The bonded bourbon is New Riff's own distillate, made at Newport, not whiskey bought from another distillery.","Bottled in bond fixes the strength at exactly 100 proof; the four years is the floor, and the bourbon may be older."],"cards":[{"id":"mash-bill","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 new-riff/mash-bill","prompt":"What is the mash bill of New Riff Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey Bottled in Bond?","answer":"65 per cent corn, 30 per cent rye and 5 per cent malted barley, by the distillery's own account."},{"id":"first-release","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 new-riff/first-release","prompt":"When did the first bourbon distilled at New Riff go on sale?","answer":"In August 2018, four years after the distillery opened at Newport in 2014, and bottled in bond."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 new-riff/mash-bill","q":"A guest asks how much rye is in New Riff's bonded bourbon. What share does the distillery give?","options":["10 per cent","30 per cent","51 per cent","20 per cent"],"a":1,"explain":"The distillery gives 65 per cent corn, 30 rye and 5 malted barley; 51 per cent is the share of corn the law asks of any bourbon."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 new-riff/first-release","q":"New Riff opened in 2014. How long did it wait before selling a bourbon of its own make?","options":["Two years","Six years","Eight years","Four years"],"a":3,"explain":"It waited the four years the bond demands and sold its first bourbon in 2018; at two years the whiskey could have been a straight bourbon, but not a bonded one."}],"sources":[{"title":"New Riff Distilling: Bottled in Bond bourbon","url":"https://newriffdistilling.com/spirits/new-riff-bottled-in-bond-bourbon/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"New Riff Distilling: Our whiskey making process","url":"https://newriffdistilling.com/our-process/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"New Riff Distilling: Our story","url":"https://newriffdistilling.com/our-story/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"New Riff Distilling shop: Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey, Bottled in Bond","url":"https://shop.newriffdistilling.com/products/new-riff-kentucky-straight-bourbon-whiskey-bottled-in-bond","kind":"primary"},{"title":"VinePair: things to know about New Riff Distilling","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/ntk-new-riff-distilling/","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"evan-williams","group":"bottled-in-bond","family":"American","producer":"Evan Williams","madeBy":"Heaven Hill","style":"bottled-in-bond","chapter":"american-labels","shelf":"","library":"","say":"","place":"Distilled in Louisville, aged and bottled in Bardstown, Kentucky","year":"1957","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"Evan Williams Bottled in Bond Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey, bottled in bond","mash":"Heaven Hill publishes no figures for this bottling; the house's traditional bourbon recipe, used here, is widely given as 78 per cent corn, 10 rye and 12 malted barley.","still":"Distilled by Heaven Hill at its Bernheim Distillery in Louisville, bought in 1999 after the Bardstown fire, all of it from one distilling season.","wood":"New charred American oak, four years under government supervision in a bonded warehouse, by the brand's account.","strength":"50% ABV (100 proof)","taste":"Bright gold, with caramel on the nose and hints of vanilla, oak and barley; then citrus over vanilla and black pepper, and a warm, long, dry finish.","pour":"Neat, on the rocks or in a highball. At 100 proof on the rail it keeps its spine in an Old Fashioned, a Manhattan or a Whiskey Sour.","label":[{"term":"Bottled-in-Bond","means":"One season, one distiller at one distillery, four years or more in a bonded warehouse, then exactly 100 proof."},{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Bourbon made in Kentucky and kept at least two years in new charred oak."}]},"table":"Evan Williams is the Heaven Hill bourbon named for an early Louisville distiller, and its Bottled in Bond is the white label at 100 proof, four years in a bonded warehouse. It pours caramel, vanilla and black pepper, the bonded bourbon to keep on a working rail.","story":"The label is named for Evan Williams (1755 to 1810), who came to Kentucky from Wales and settled at Louisville. A state historical marker there says he built a distillery on the Ohio River in 1783 and that it is said to have been the first commercial distillery in Kentucky; historians have questioned both the date and the claim, so they are told as tradition. The bourbon came much later. Heaven Hill was founded at Bardstown in 1935 by five brothers of a local family and a group of investors, and it introduced Evan Williams in 1957. On 7 November 1996 fire destroyed its Bardstown distillery and seven of its warehouses, and in 1999 Heaven Hill bought the Bernheim Distillery in Louisville, which from then on distilled the bourbon for ageing and bottling at Bardstown. Beside the black label at 86 proof stands the white label, the brand's bottle of the bond. Behind a bar it is the bonded bourbon a working rail can pour all night: the house recipe, held to one season, four years and 100 proof.","others":[{"name":"Evan Williams Black Label","note":"The everyday bottling of the same name, at 86 proof and without the bond."},{"name":"Evan Williams Single Barrel","note":"A vintage dated bourbon, one barrel to a bottling, with the day the barrel was filled on the label."}],"traps":["The year 1783 is the tradition's date for the man's distillery, not the bourbon's: Heaven Hill first sold Evan Williams in 1957.","Only the white label is bottled in bond; the black label at 86 proof makes none of the bond's promises.","Evan Williams is made by Heaven Hill, distilled at Bernheim in Louisville: no Evan Williams firm distils it."],"cards":[{"id":"white-label","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 evan-williams/white-label","prompt":"What sets the Evan Williams white label apart from the black label?","answer":"It is bottled in bond: one season, one distillery, four years in a bonded warehouse, and 100 proof, not 86."},{"id":"namesake","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 evan-williams/namesake","prompt":"Who was the Evan Williams of the label, and what is claimed for him?","answer":"A Welsh settler of Louisville (1755 to 1810) said to have built a distillery on the Ohio in 1783; the date is tradition."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 evan-williams/white-label","q":"A guest asks at what strength the Evan Williams Bottled in Bond is poured. What does the bond fix?","options":["86 proof","80 proof","90 proof","100 proof"],"a":3,"explain":"The bond fixes the strength at exactly 100 proof; 86 proof is the black label, which is not bonded."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 evan-williams/namesake","q":"Tradition says the man the Evan Williams label is named for built a distillery on the Ohio at Louisville. In which year?","options":["1957","1783","1935","1810"],"a":1,"explain":"The state marker gives 1783, a date historians question and the record tells as tradition; 1957 is the year Heaven Hill first sold the bourbon, and 1810 the year he died."}],"sources":[{"title":"Evan Williams: Bottled-in-Bond","url":"https://evanwilliams.com/bottled-in-bond-bourbon","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Heaven Hill Distillery: Evan Williams Bottled-in-Bond","url":"https://heavenhilldistillery.com/ew-bottled-in-bond.php","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Kentucky Historical Society: Evan Williams marker","url":"https://history.ky.gov/markers/evan-williams-1755-1810","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Heaven Hill: Through the years","url":"https://heavenhill.com/through-the-years/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Evan Williams bourbon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evan_Williams_(bourbon)","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"heaven-hill","group":"bottled-in-bond","family":"American","producer":"Heaven Hill","madeBy":"","style":"bottled-in-bond","chapter":"american-labels","shelf":"","library":"Heaven Hill","say":"","place":"Bardstown, Nelson County, Kentucky","year":"1935","yearIs":"founded","bottle":{"name":"Heaven Hill Bottled-in-Bond 7 Year Old Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey, bottled in bond","mash":"By Heaven Hill's own account 78 per cent corn, 12 per cent malted barley and 10 per cent rye, the house's traditional bourbon recipe.","still":"Distilled by Heaven Hill at its Bernheim Distillery in Louisville in a single distilling season, as the bond requires, then aged in the house's warehouses.","wood":"Seven years in new charred American oak, three more than the bond asks; the age is stated on the front label.","strength":"50% ABV (100 proof)","taste":"Amber in the glass, with vanilla, honey and caramel on the nose, then a sweet palate edged with a light rye spice, and a long finish.","pour":"Neat or with a few drops of water, so the seven years show; it also builds a sturdy Old Fashioned and a Manhattan.","label":[{"term":"This Bourbon is 7 Years Old","means":"The age statement: nothing in the bottle has spent fewer than seven years in oak."},{"term":"Bottled-in-Bond","means":"One distilling season at one distillery, at least four years in a bonded warehouse, and exactly 100 proof."}]},"table":"Heaven Hill is the family distillery of Bardstown, Kentucky, and its namesake Bottled-in-Bond 7 Year Old carries the bond and an age statement on one label. At 100 proof it pours vanilla, honey and caramel, with a little oak and rye spice.","story":"Heaven Hill filled its first barrel at Bardstown on 13 December 1935, as the Old Heavenhill Springs distillery, founded by five brothers of a Bardstown family and a group of local investors; the brothers soon bought their partners out. The name, the company says, is that of the farmer who had owned the land, split in two by a clerk's slip on the paperwork sent to Frankfort and never mended. The house grew into a maker of many labels, among them Evan Williams from 1957. On 7 November 1996 fire swept seven of its warehouses and destroyed the Bardstown distillery, and in 1999 Heaven Hill bought the Bernheim Distillery in Louisville and moved its distilling there. For years a six year old bonded bourbon under the house's own name was sold only in Kentucky; it was retired in 2018, and in June 2019 Heaven Hill announced the 7 Year Old in its place, the same recipe with a year more in oak. In 2025 the house filled the first barrel at a new distillery in Bardstown, Heaven Hill Springs. For a bartender it shows the two promises a label can stack: the bond, and an age stated in words on the front.","others":[{"name":"Heaven Hill Heritage Collection","note":"Much older bourbon under the house's own name, released in editions; the 2024 edition was eighteen years old."}],"traps":["The 7 Year Old took the place of a six year old bonded bottling sold only in Kentucky, which was retired in 2018.","Bottled in bond promises at least four years; the seven on this label is Heaven Hill's own age statement on top of the bond.","Heaven Hill makes many labels, Evan Williams, Elijah Craig, Larceny and Henry McKenna among them; this one carries the house's own name."],"cards":[{"id":"age-statement","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 heaven-hill/age-statement","prompt":"How many years beyond the bond's minimum has Heaven Hill Bottled-in-Bond 7 Year Old spent in oak?","answer":"Three: seven years in new charred oak, where the bond asks at least four."},{"id":"fire","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 heaven-hill/fire","prompt":"What destroyed Heaven Hill's Bardstown distillery in 1996?","answer":"Fire, on 7 November 1996, which took seven warehouses too; from 1999 the house distilled at Bernheim in Louisville."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 heaven-hill/age-statement","q":"A guest reads the Heaven Hill bonded label, which states seven years. What is the minimum age the bond itself requires?","options":["Four years","Two years","Six years","Ten years"],"a":0,"explain":"The bond asks at least four years, and seven is the house's own statement on top of it; two years is the floor for a straight bourbon, not a bonded one."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 heaven-hill/fire","q":"In which year did fire destroy Heaven Hill's distillery at Bardstown?","options":["1935","1999","1996","2019"],"a":2,"explain":"The fire came on 7 November 1996; 1999 is the year Heaven Hill bought the Bernheim Distillery in Louisville."}],"sources":[{"title":"Heaven Hill Distillery: Heaven Hill Bottled-in-Bond","url":"https://heavenhilldistillery.com/hh-bottled-in-bond.php","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Heaven Hill: the launch of the namesake Bottled-in-Bond bourbon","url":"https://heavenhill.com/press-detail.php?postid=heaven-hill-distillery-launches-historic-bottled-in-bond-namesake-bourbon","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Heaven Hill: Through the years","url":"https://heavenhill.com/through-the-years/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Heaven Hill","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heaven_Hill","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Gear Patrol: Heaven Hill brings back its bonded bourbon at seven years","url":"https://gearpatrol.com/2019/06/24/heaven-hill-bottled-in-bond-7-year-bourbon/","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"henry-mckenna","group":"bottled-in-bond","family":"American","producer":"Henry McKenna","madeBy":"Heaven Hill","style":"bottled-in-bond","chapter":"american-labels","shelf":"","library":"","say":"","place":"Distilled in Louisville, aged in Bardstown, Kentucky","year":"1855","yearIs":"founded","bottle":{"name":"Henry McKenna 10 Year Single Barrel Bottled in Bond Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey, single barrel, bottled in bond","mash":"By Heaven Hill's own account the recipe of its namesake bonded bourbon: 78 per cent corn, 12 per cent malted barley and 10 per cent rye.","still":"Distilled by Heaven Hill at its Bernheim Distillery in Louisville; each bottling is one barrel from one distilling season.","wood":"Ten years in new charred American oak, the age stated on the label; each barrel is chosen and bottled alone, never mingled with others.","strength":"50% ABV (100 proof)","taste":"Bottles differ by barrel; usually vanilla, caramel and oak on the nose with a light herbal note, then oak, sharp spice and honey, and a long, sweet, spicy finish.","pour":"Neat in a tulip glass with water beside it; ten years of oak make it a bourbon to sip first, though it builds a firm Old Fashioned.","label":[{"term":"Single Barrel","means":"Every bottle comes from one barrel, never mingled; the term has no legal definition, so bottles differ."},{"term":"Barreled On","means":"Written by hand on the front with the barrel's number: the day the barrel was filled."},{"term":"Bottled-In-Bond","means":"One season at one distillery, four years or more in a bonded warehouse, bottled at exactly 100 proof."}]},"table":"Henry McKenna is the Heaven Hill bourbon named for an Irish distiller of Nelson County, and its 10 Year Single Barrel Bottled in Bond joins three promises on one label: ten years, one barrel and the bond. It pours vanilla, caramel, honey and spice.","story":"Henry McKenna (1819 to 1893) came to Kentucky from County Derry in Ireland and settled at Fairfield, a small town north east of Bardstown in Nelson County. There he went into business, and his whiskey, sold under his own name, came from a farm distillery that the label dates to 1855, the year its face prints. After his death in 1893 his sons carried the distillery on, and in 1941 the family sold it and the brand to Joseph E. Seagram and Sons. The domestic rights to the name later passed to Heaven Hill, a few miles away in Bardstown. In 1994, by Heaven Hill's account, the house added a single barrel bottling of the label, ten years old and bottled in bond, each bottle marked by hand with its barrel number and the day the barrel was filled. From 1999 Heaven Hill distilled it at its Bernheim Distillery in Louisville. For a bartender it is the one bottle that teaches three terms at once: an age statement, a single barrel and the bond, and the handwritten barrel number makes the second plain to a guest.","others":[{"name":"Henry McKenna Sour Mash","note":"The label's everyday bourbon at 80 proof, with no age, barrel or bond on its face."}],"traps":["The ten years is the label's own statement; the bond asks only four.","Single barrel means one barrel to a bottling, so two bottles may differ: read the barrel number on the label.","The bourbon is not made at Fairfield, where Henry McKenna distilled: Heaven Hill makes it at Bernheim in Louisville."],"cards":[{"id":"barrel-number","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 henry-mckenna/barrel-number","prompt":"What is written by hand on the front of Henry McKenna 10 Year Single Barrel Bottled in Bond?","answer":"The barrel's number and the day that barrel was filled, both in handwriting."},{"id":"namesake","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 henry-mckenna/namesake","prompt":"Who was Henry McKenna, and where did he make his whiskey?","answer":"An Irish distiller from County Derry (1819 to 1893) who settled at Fairfield in Nelson County, Kentucky."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 henry-mckenna/barrel-number","q":"A guest asks what the handwritten line on the front of a Henry McKenna single barrel bottle records. What is it?","options":["The bottling day","Barrel number and fill date","Rickhouse and floor","The bottler's initials"],"a":1,"explain":"Each label carries the barrel's number and the day it was filled, in handwriting; the bottling day is not on that line, which dates the barrel, not the bottle."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 henry-mckenna/namesake","q":"Henry McKenna, the Irishman behind the label, settled and distilled in which Kentucky town?","options":["Bardstown","Loretto","Frankfort","Fairfield"],"a":3,"explain":"He settled at Fairfield in Nelson County; Bardstown is the nearby town where Heaven Hill ages the bourbon."}],"sources":[{"title":"Heaven Hill Distillery: Henry McKenna Single Barrel","url":"https://heavenhilldistillery.com/henry-mckenna-single-barrel.php","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Heaven Hill: new packaging for the Henry McKenna bourbons","url":"https://heavenhill.com/news-and-notes/heaven-hill-distilleries-unveils-new-packaging-for-henry-mckenna-bourbon-family/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Kentucky Distillers' Association: 160 years of H. McKenna whiskey","url":"https://kybourbon.com/enthusiast-stories/160-years-of-h-mckenna-medicinal-whiskey/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"VinePair: things to know about Henry McKenna","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/ntk-henry-mckenna-bourbon-guide/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Whiskey University: Henry McKenna","url":"https://whiskeyuniv.com/henry-mckenna","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"blantons","group":"small-batch-single-barrel","family":"American","producer":"Blanton's","madeBy":"Buffalo Trace Distillery","style":"small-batch-single-barrel","chapter":"american-labels","shelf":"","library":"","say":"","place":"Frankfort, Franklin County, Kentucky","year":"1984","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"Blanton's Original Single Barrel Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey","mash":"Corn, rye and malted barley to the distillery's Mash Bill No. 2, the recipe it names for Blanton's; no percentages are published.","still":"Distilled at Buffalo Trace Distillery in Frankfort; the producer's pages name the recipe and the warehouse, not the stills.","wood":"New charred American oak, aged in Warehouse H, a metal-clad warehouse built in 1935 whose walls follow the heat and cold of the seasons closely, by the brand's account. No age statement.","strength":"46.5% ABV (93 proof)","taste":"Nutmeg and baking spice on the nose; dry vanilla with honey, caramel and corn on the palate, and a medium finish where corn and nutmeg come back. Each barrel reads a little differently.","pour":"Neat or with a few drops of water, so that one barrel can be tasted on its own terms; over one large cube for a slower glass, or stirred into an Old Fashioned.","label":[{"term":"Single Barrel","means":"Every bottle is drawn from one barrel alone, never mingled with another; the words have no legal definition."},{"term":"Dumped on","means":"The handwritten date the barrel was emptied, the day its ageing stopped; bottling may follow later."},{"term":"Warehouse H","means":"The metal-clad warehouse in Frankfort where every barrel of Blanton's rests."},{"term":"Barrel No. and Rick No.","means":"The number of the barrel and of the rack it rested on, written by hand."}]},"table":"Blanton's is the single barrel bourbon of Buffalo Trace in Frankfort, every bottle drawn from one barrel aged in the metal-clad Warehouse H. Blanton's Original Single Barrel, at 93 proof under its horse and jockey stopper, pours honey, caramel, dry vanilla and nutmeg.","story":"Albert Bacon Blanton (1881 to 1959) was born on a farm near the Frankfort distillery and joined it in 1897, at sixteen, as an office boy. He rose to be its president in 1921 and led it until 1952; after the repeal of Prohibition he had Warehouse H built in metal, quick to raise, in 1935. When he entertained important guests, the story goes, he drew whiskey for them from chosen barrels in the centre of that warehouse. Elmer T. Lee (1919 to 2013), then master distiller, remembered that habit, and in 1984, near the end of his career, he launched a bourbon bottled one barrel at a time and named it for the colonel: Blanton's Single Barrel, which the distillery calls the first single barrel bourbon. Each bottle carries its barrel number and the day the barrel was emptied, written by hand, under a horse and jockey stopper; since 1999 there have been eight of them, each marked with one letter of the name. The distillery took the name Buffalo Trace in 1999. Behind a bar Blanton's is the bottle that taught guests to ask for a single barrel, and the proof that no two barrels read quite alike.","others":[{"name":"Blanton's Gold","note":"A stronger single barrel bottling of the same bourbon."},{"name":"Blanton's Straight from the Barrel","note":"Single barrels bottled uncut, at the strength they leave the cask."}],"traps":["There is no Blanton's distillery: the bourbon is made and aged at Buffalo Trace in Frankfort, in Warehouse H.","The letter and the pose on the stopper are placed at random; they say nothing about the whiskey in the bottle.","Single barrel is not barrel proof: the Original is brought down with water to 93 proof."],"cards":[{"id":"dump-date","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 blantons/dump-date","prompt":"What does the handwritten date on a Blanton's label record?","answer":"The dump date: the day its one barrel was emptied and the bourbon stopped ageing."},{"id":"namesake","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 blantons/namesake","prompt":"Who is Blanton's named for?","answer":"Albert Bacon Blanton (1881 to 1959), president of the Frankfort distillery from 1921; Elmer T. Lee launched the bourbon in his honour in 1984."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 blantons/dump-date","q":"A guest points to the date written by hand on a bottle of Blanton's. What does it mark?","options":["The day the barrel was filled","The day the barrel was emptied","The day the bottle was corked","The day the whiskey was distilled"],"a":1,"explain":"The label records the dump date, the day the barrel was emptied and its ageing stopped; bottling can follow later, so it is not the day the bottle was corked."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 blantons/namesake","q":"In which year did Elmer T. Lee launch Blanton's Single Barrel?","options":["1935","1959","1984","1999"],"a":2,"explain":"Blanton's went on sale in 1984; 1935 is the year Warehouse H was built and 1999 the year the distillery became Buffalo Trace."}],"sources":[{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: Blanton's Original Single Barrel","url":"https://www.buffalotracedistillery.com/our-brands/blantons-single-barrel/blantons-original-single-barrel/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: Albert B. Blanton","url":"https://www.buffalotracedistillery.com/our-distillery/legendary-people/colonel-albert-blanton/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Blanton's: Questions and answers","url":"https://blantonsbourbon.com/pages/faq","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Blanton's","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blanton%27s","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: a guide to Blanton's bourbon","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/whiskey-articles/the-ultimate-guide-to-blantons-bourbon/","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"eagle-rare","group":"small-batch-single-barrel","family":"American","producer":"Eagle Rare","madeBy":"Buffalo Trace Distillery","style":"small-batch-single-barrel","chapter":"american-labels","shelf":"","library":"","say":"","place":"Frankfort, Franklin County, Kentucky","year":"1975","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"Eagle Rare 10 Year Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey","mash":"Corn, rye and malted barley to the distillery's Mash Bill No. 1, the recipe of Buffalo Trace bourbon; no percentages are published.","still":"Distilled at Buffalo Trace Distillery in Frankfort; the producer's pages name the recipe and not the stills.","wood":"New charred American oak for no less than ten years, by the distillery's account; the age statement stands on the back label.","strength":"45% ABV (90 proof)","taste":"Toffee, orange peel, herbs, honey, leather and oak on the nose; a bold, dry palate of candied almond and dark cocoa, and a long, dry finish.","pour":"Neat or with a little water; it holds its own in a Manhattan or an Old Fashioned, where the ten years of oak show through the vermouth or the sugar.","label":[{"term":"Aged 10 Years","means":"The age statement, on the back label: nothing in the bottle is younger than ten years."},{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Bourbon made in Kentucky and aged at least two years in new charred oak."}]},"table":"Eagle Rare is the ten year old Kentucky straight bourbon of Buffalo Trace in Frankfort, made from the same recipe as Buffalo Trace bourbon and left far longer in oak. Eagle Rare 10 Year pours toffee, orange peel, leather and dark cocoa at 90 proof.","story":"Seagram introduced Eagle Rare in 1975 as a ten year old Kentucky straight bourbon at 101 proof, made, by most accounts, at its Four Roses distillery in Lawrenceburg. The Sazerac Company bought the label from Seagram in 1989, and in 1992 bought the Frankfort distillery that it renamed Buffalo Trace in 1999, where the bourbon is made. In 2005 the strength came down to 90 proof and the bottling was sold as a single barrel bourbon, each bottle drawn from one barrel. About 2012, by later accounts, a new automated bottling line came into use; because, as the distillery explained, it can let a little of one barrel run into the next, the words Single Barrel came off the label, and the bottle was redesigned with the age statement moved to the back. The distillery describes every barrel as chosen with care, and the whiskey kept its recipe and its ten years. For a bartender it is the lesson in reading a label as it stands: ten years of oak and one recipe, with no claim to a single barrel.","others":[{"name":"Eagle Rare 17 Year Old","note":"The same recipe after seventeen years in oak, a yearly release of the Buffalo Trace Antique Collection."}],"traps":["Eagle Rare 10 Year no longer prints Single Barrel on its label: the words came off about 2012, with a new bottling line.","The 101 proof was the strength of the Seagram bottling of 1975 and the years after; the bourbon has been 90 proof since 2005.","It shares Mash Bill No. 1 with Buffalo Trace bourbon; Blanton's is made from Mash Bill No. 2."],"cards":[{"id":"mash-bill","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 eagle-rare/mash-bill","prompt":"Which Buffalo Trace mash bill goes into Eagle Rare 10 Year?","answer":"Mash Bill No. 1, the recipe of Buffalo Trace bourbon; Blanton's is made from Mash Bill No. 2."},{"id":"label-change","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 eagle-rare/label-change","prompt":"Why does Eagle Rare 10 Year no longer print Single Barrel on its label?","answer":"About 2012 a new automated bottling line came in that can let one barrel run into the next, so the words came off."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 eagle-rare/mash-bill","q":"Eagle Rare 10 Year and Blanton's come from one Frankfort distillery. Which recipe goes into Eagle Rare?","options":["The wheated Weller recipe","Mash Bill No. 2","The Sazerac rye recipe","Mash Bill No. 1"],"a":3,"explain":"Eagle Rare is made from Mash Bill No. 1, the recipe of Buffalo Trace bourbon; Mash Bill No. 2 is the recipe of Blanton's."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 eagle-rare/label-change","q":"Why did the words Single Barrel come off the Eagle Rare label about 2012?","options":["A new bottling line can mix barrels","A new law barred the words on bourbon","The bourbon became a small batch blend","Seagram kept the right to the words"],"a":0,"explain":"The automated line that came in can let a little of one barrel run into the next, so the claim was dropped; no law barred the words, and Blanton's prints them."}],"sources":[{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: Eagle Rare 10","url":"https://www.buffalotracedistillery.com/our-brands/eagle-rare/eagle-rare-10/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: Eagle Rare 17 Year Old letter of 2025","url":"https://cms.buffalotracedistillery.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Eagle-Rare-17yr-Letter-2025.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Eagle Rare","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eagle_Rare","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"VinePair: things to know about Eagle Rare","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/ntk-eagle-rare-bourbon/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: a guide to Eagle Rare 10 Year Old","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/whiskey-styles/american-whiskey/the-ultimate-guide-to-eagle-rare-10-year-old-bourbon/","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"knob-creek","group":"small-batch-single-barrel","family":"American","producer":"Knob Creek","madeBy":"Jim Beam","style":"small-batch-single-barrel","chapter":"american-labels","shelf":"","library":"","say":"","place":"Clermont, Bullitt County, Kentucky","year":"1992","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"Knob Creek Small Batch 9 Year Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey","mash":"Corn, rye and malted barley, with corn the larger part as the law requires; Beam publishes no percentages for it.","still":"Distilled by Beam in Kentucky and, by the producer's account, taken off the still at a lower proof; it names no single still house for the bottling.","wood":"New charred American oak for at least nine years. The age statement, taken off in late 2016, has been back on the label since 2020.","strength":"50% ABV (100 proof)","taste":"Toasted nuts, grain and oak on the nose; a full, sweet and woody palate with caramel and vanilla, and a long finish.","pour":"Neat with a splash of water, or over one large cube; at 100 proof it stands up to vermouth and bitters in a Manhattan or a Boulevardier.","label":[{"term":"Small Batch","means":"No legal meaning in the United States: the producer's word for a chosen mingling of barrels."},{"term":"100 Proof","means":"Fifty per cent alcohol by volume, the strength of the bonded whiskeys it was modelled on."},{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Bourbon made in Kentucky and aged at least two years in new charred oak; this one nine."}]},"table":"Knob Creek is Beam's small batch bourbon from Kentucky, made in the manner of the full strength whiskey sold before Prohibition. Knob Creek Small Batch 9 Year spends at least nine years in charred oak and is bottled at 100 proof: toasted nuts, caramel and oak.","story":"Booker Noe (1929 to 2004), a grandson of Jim Beam, was the family distillery's master distiller when he created Knob Creek in 1992. It was one of the Small Batch Bourbon Collection, four bourbons beside Booker's, Baker's and Basil Hayden's, and he built it, by the producer's account, on the bonded whiskey of the years before Prohibition, the kind the Bottled-in-Bond Act of 1897 stood behind: long in oak and bottled at 100 proof. The name is a place, not a person. Knob Creek is the stream that ran by the Kentucky farm where Abraham Lincoln (1809 to 1865) spent his boyhood; in 1860 he wrote that his memories began at the Knob Creek place. In late 2016 the producer took the nine year statement off the label, saying it could not promise that every barrel had reached nine years, and in 2020 the statement came back. For a bartender Knob Creek is a steady 100 proof bourbon for the stirred drinks, and a plain lesson that the words small batch say nothing of age: the number on the label does.","others":[{"name":"Knob Creek Single Barrel Reserve 9 Year","note":"One barrel bottled on its own, also nine years old, at a higher strength."},{"name":"Knob Creek Rye","note":"A straight rye whiskey at 100 proof, first released in 2012; a seven year age statement came onto its label in 2023."}],"traps":["Small batch has no legal meaning in the United States: no rule sets how many barrels make a batch.","Knob Creek is named for the creek by Abraham Lincoln's boyhood farm, not for the place where he was born.","Bottles filled between late 2016 and 2020 carry no age statement; that is the label of those years, not a different bourbon."],"cards":[{"id":"age-statement","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 knob-creek/age-statement","prompt":"In which year did the nine year age statement return to the Knob Creek label?","answer":"In 2020, after the producer took it off in late 2016 for want of enough nine year old barrels."},{"id":"name","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 knob-creek/name","prompt":"What is Knob Creek named for?","answer":"The creek by the Kentucky farm where Abraham Lincoln spent his boyhood; Booker Noe created the bourbon in 1992."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 knob-creek/age-statement","q":"A guest holds a Knob Creek bottle with no age on its label. Between which years was the nine year statement left off?","options":["Late 2016 to 2020","2001 to 2005","1992 to 1997","2012 to 2016"],"a":0,"explain":"The statement came off in late 2016 and returned in 2020; 1992 is the year the bourbon was created, with its nine years printed."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 knob-creek/name","q":"Knob Creek names a place rather than a person. What is it?","options":["The town where Lincoln was born","The Beam family's first farm","A creek by Lincoln's boyhood farm","A hill above the Clermont stills"],"a":2,"explain":"It is the creek by the farm where Lincoln spent his boyhood; he was born at another farm, Sinking Spring, so the town of his birth is the common mistake."}],"sources":[{"title":"Beam Distilling: Knob Creek Bourbon","url":"https://www.beamdistilling.com/our-brands/knob-creek-bourbon","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Beam founders: the Knob Creek story","url":"https://founders.beamsuntory.com/dna/knobcreek","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Knob Creek bourbon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knob_Creek_(bourbon)","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: Knob Creek gets its nine year statement back","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/bourbon/knob-creek-to-get-its-9-year-age-statement-back/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Whisky Advocate: Beam in his blood","url":"https://whiskyadvocate.com/booker-noe-beam-blood","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"elijah-craig","group":"small-batch-single-barrel","family":"American","producer":"Elijah Craig","madeBy":"Heaven Hill","style":"small-batch-single-barrel","chapter":"american-labels","shelf":"","library":"","say":"","place":"Bardstown, Nelson County, Kentucky","year":"1986","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"Elijah Craig Small Batch Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey","mash":"Corn, rye and malted barley; Heaven Hill gives no percentages for this bottling.","still":"Made by Heaven Hill, which has distilled its whiskey at the Bernheim Distillery in Louisville since 1999, after fire took its Bardstown distillery in 1996; the barrels age around Bardstown.","wood":"New oak barrels with a level 3 char, by the house's account: a mingling of barrels of eight to twelve years, in batches of 200 barrels or fewer. No age statement since 2016.","strength":"47% ABV (94 proof)","taste":"Vanilla bean, sweet fruit and fresh mint on the nose; warm on the palate, with spice, a little smoke and nutmeg, and a long, sweet, gently toasted finish.","pour":"Neat or with a few drops of water, or on one large cube; with its 94 proof and long years of oak it makes a deep Old Fashioned and a firm Whiskey Sour.","label":[{"term":"Small Batch","means":"By the house's account a mingling of no more than 200 barrels; the words have no legal definition."},{"term":"94 Proof","means":"Forty-seven per cent alcohol by volume, the bottling's one strength."}]},"table":"Elijah Craig is the Heaven Hill bourbon named for a Baptist minister of early Kentucky. Elijah Craig Small Batch mingles barrels of eight to twelve years at 94 proof: vanilla, nutmeg and toasted oak, with the depth of its years for an Old Fashioned.","story":"Elijah Craig (died 1808) was a Baptist minister from Virginia who settled in Kentucky, where he ran a mill at Royal Spring, at what became Georgetown. The claim that he made the first bourbon there in 1789, or first charred his barrels, goes back to a history of Kentucky published in 1874, and historians treat it as legend. Heaven Hill of Bardstown chose his name for a new label in 1986: a twelve year old small batch bourbon. Fire destroyed the Bardstown distillery in 1996, and from 1999 the house distilled at Bernheim in Louisville while its whiskey went on ageing around Bardstown. To stretch its stock of older barrels as the label grew, Heaven Hill took the twelve year statement off the back label in 2016, and the Small Batch became a mingling of barrels of eight to twelve years at the same 94 proof. For a bartender it is the bottle that shows what long years in oak bring to a small batch bourbon, with a founding story to tell as the legend it is.","others":[{"name":"Elijah Craig Barrel Proof","note":"Twelve years old and bottled uncut, in batches through the year; it kept its age statement."},{"name":"Elijah Craig Toasted Barrel","note":"The Small Batch given a second spell in a toasted new oak barrel."}],"traps":["Elijah Craig did not invent bourbon: the claim goes back to a history of 1874 and is told as legend.","The Small Batch has carried no age statement since 2016; the twelve years belong to older bottles and to the Barrel Proof.","Heaven Hill distils in Louisville, at Bernheim, not in Bardstown: the Bardstown distillery burned in 1996."],"cards":[{"id":"age-change","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 elijah-craig/age-change","prompt":"What did Elijah Craig Small Batch take off its label in 2016?","answer":"Its twelve year age statement; it became a mingling of barrels of eight to twelve years, at the same 94 proof."},{"id":"namesake","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 elijah-craig/namesake","prompt":"Who was the Elijah Craig of Heaven Hill's bourbon?","answer":"A Baptist minister of early Kentucky (died 1808), credited by legend with inventing bourbon."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 elijah-craig/age-change","q":"A guest remembers Elijah Craig Small Batch as a twelve year old. What ages of barrel go into it since 2016?","options":["Four to six years","Eight to twelve years","Twelve to fifteen years","Two to four years"],"a":1,"explain":"The house mingles barrels of eight to twelve years; twelve to fifteen would match the old age statement or more, which the label no longer makes."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 elijah-craig/namesake","q":"Heaven Hill's label names a figure of early Kentucky. Who was Elijah Craig?","options":["A Louisville merchant","A Bardstown banker","A Frankfort senator","A Baptist minister"],"a":3,"explain":"Elijah Craig was a Baptist minister from Virginia who settled in Kentucky and ran a mill at Royal Spring; the legend that he made the first bourbon is told of the minister, not of a merchant or a banker."}],"sources":[{"title":"Elijah Craig: Small Batch","url":"https://elijahcraig.com/small-batch","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Heaven Hill: Through the years","url":"https://heavenhill.com/through-the-years/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Heaven Hill: the Small Batch age change","url":"https://heavenhill.com/news-and-notes/elijah-craig-small-batch-age-change/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Elijah Craig bourbon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elijah_Craig_(bourbon)","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Encyclopedia Virginia: Elijah Craig","url":"https://encyclopediavirginia.org/entries/elijah-craig-ca-1745-may-18-1808/","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"russells-reserve","group":"small-batch-single-barrel","family":"American","producer":"Russell's Reserve","madeBy":"Wild Turkey","style":"small-batch-single-barrel","chapter":"american-labels","shelf":"","library":"","say":"","place":"Lawrenceburg, Anderson County, Kentucky","year":"2001","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"Russell's Reserve Single Barrel Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey","mash":"Wild Turkey's bourbon recipe of corn, rye and malted barley, with corn the larger part; the house publishes no percentages.","still":"Distilled at the Wild Turkey Distillery in Lawrenceburg on a column still and then a doubler, as the house's bourbons are.","wood":"New American oak with the house's deep number four char, the alligator char; each bottle from one barrel, and no age statement on the label.","strength":"55% ABV (110 proof)","taste":"Vanilla, nuts and brown sugar on the nose with oak and cinnamon; a bold, spicy palate of toffee, liquorice and vanilla, and a finish of sweet spice with a malty note.","pour":"Neat, with a few drops of water to open the strength, or over one large cube; it gives a firm backbone to an Old Fashioned.","label":[{"term":"Single Barrel","means":"Each bottle comes from one barrel, chosen by the house; the words have no legal definition."},{"term":"110 Proof","means":"Fifty-five per cent alcohol by volume, the one strength every barrel is brought down to."}]},"table":"Russell's Reserve is the Wild Turkey label a son first made in 1998 to honour his father, then master distiller at Lawrenceburg. Russell's Reserve Single Barrel comes one barrel at a time, not chill filtered, at 110 proof: toffee, vanilla, cinnamon and oak, with more weight than Wild Turkey 101.","story":"Russell's Reserve began as a son's tribute to his father, then master distiller of the Wild Turkey Distillery at Lawrenceburg. By the brand's account, his son made a small batch bourbon in 1998 to mark his father's forty-five years at the distillery, and called it Russell's Reserve. The label came to market in 2001 as a ten year old Kentucky straight bourbon at 101 proof, the strength of the house's flagship, and in 2005 the ten year old was brought down to 90 proof. In 2013 came Russell's Reserve Single Barrel, each bottle from one barrel, bottled at 110 proof and without chill filtering. The son, who had joined the distillery in 1981, was named master distiller beside his father in 2015. Behind a bar it shows the Wild Turkey style at its plainest: deep char, a low barrel entry, one cask at a time, and the strength left high.","others":[{"name":"Russell's Reserve 10 Year","note":"The ten year old small batch bourbon at 90 proof, the bottling where the label began."},{"name":"Russell's Reserve Single Barrel Rye","note":"The same one barrel at a time approach, for rye whiskey."}],"traps":["Russell's Reserve is made at the Wild Turkey Distillery in Lawrenceburg: there is no separate Russell's Reserve distillery.","The 110 proof is a set bottling strength, not barrel proof: every barrel is brought down to the same figure.","The label of 1998 and 2001 was a ten year old small batch; the Single Barrel came only in 2013."],"cards":[{"id":"proof","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 russells-reserve/proof","prompt":"At what proof is Russell's Reserve Single Barrel bottled?","answer":"110 proof, 55 per cent, one barrel at a time and not chill filtered."},{"id":"namesake","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 russells-reserve/namesake","prompt":"Whom does the Russell's Reserve label honour?","answer":"A father and son who have both been master distiller at Wild Turkey; by the brand's account the son made it in 1998 for his father's forty-five years there."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 russells-reserve/proof","q":"A guest compares Russell's Reserve Single Barrel with Wild Turkey 101. At what proof is the single barrel bottled?","options":["90 proof","101 proof","104 proof","110 proof"],"a":3,"explain":"The Single Barrel is bottled at 110 proof, not chill filtered; 101 is the proof of Wild Turkey 101 and of the Russell's Reserve of 2001."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 russells-reserve/namesake","q":"In which year did Russell's Reserve first reach the market, as a ten year old at 101 proof?","options":["1998","2001","2005","2013"],"a":1,"explain":"It came to market in 2001; 1998 is the year, by the brand's account, the son first made it for his father, and 2013 the year of the Single Barrel."}],"sources":[{"title":"Russell's Reserve: Single Barrel Bourbon","url":"https://www.russellsreserve.com/en-us/our-products/single-barrel-bourbon/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Russell's Reserve: Our story","url":"https://www.russellsreserve.com/our-story/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wild Turkey: Russell's Reserve Single Barrel Bourbon","url":"https://www.wildturkeybourbon.com/en-au/products/russells-reserve-single-barrel-bourbon/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Difford's Guide: Russell's Reserve Single Barrel","url":"https://www.diffordsguide.com/beer-wine-spirits/3634/wild-turkey-russells-reserve-single-barrel","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Cigar Aficionado: a first taste of Russell's Reserve Single Barrel","url":"https://www.cigaraficionado.com/article/first-taste-russells-reserve-single-barrel-16920","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"bookers","group":"barrel-proof","family":"American","producer":"Booker's","madeBy":"Jim Beam","style":"barrel-proof","chapter":"american-labels","shelf":"","library":"","say":"","place":"Clermont, Bullitt County, Kentucky","year":"1988","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"Booker's Bourbon","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey, bottled uncut at barrel proof","mash":"Jim Beam's standard bourbon recipe, given on the producer's own pages as 77 per cent corn, 13 per cent rye and 10 per cent malted barley.","still":"Distilled by Jim Beam on its Clermont campus in Kentucky, by the producer's account; no details of the equipment are given for this bottling.","wood":"New charred American oak, the barrels drawn, as the house tells it, from the centre of the rackhouse. Batches run about six to eight years, each age given by the producer.","strength":"Varies by batch, uncut, about 121 to 131 proof","taste":"Vanilla and oak on the nose, often with brown sugar, dried fruit or toasted nuts; then an intense, hot palate that turns sweet, and a long, warm finish.","pour":"Neat with water beside it, or over one large cube; a few drops of water open it. At this strength it carries a robust Old Fashioned.","label":[{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Bourbon made in Kentucky and aged at least two years in new charred oak."},{"term":"Batch","means":"The year and the batch's number within that year; since about 2015 each batch also carries a name."}]},"table":"Booker's is the barrel proof bourbon Jim Beam bottles uncut and unfiltered, from barrels drawn from the centre of its Kentucky rackhouses. Booker's Bourbon comes in named batches of about six to eight years, each at its own proof: vanilla, oak and brown sugar, hot until water opens it.","story":"Booker Noe (1929 to 2004), Jim Beam's grandson and for decades the distillery's master distiller, liked his bourbon as it came out of the barrel. For years, as the house tells it, he bottled it uncut and unfiltered from barrels at the centre of the rackhouse and gave it to friends and family at the holidays. Beam dates his introduction of Booker's True Barrel Bourbon to 1987, and most accounts give 1988 for its first general sale. In 1992 Beam gathered it with Baker's, Basil Hayden's and Knob Creek as its Small Batch Bourbon Collection. He laid barrels down to the end: rye he filled in 2003, the year before his death, came out in 2016 as Booker's Rye. Since about 2015 each batch has carried a name, often drawn from his life, his home town of Springfield or his sayings, beside the year and number on the label. For a bartender, Booker's is where barrel proof is taught: one recipe, no water after the barrel, and a strength that changes with every batch, so the label in the hand is the one figure to quote.","others":[{"name":"Booker's Rye","note":"Rye Booker Noe laid down in 2003, bottled uncut in 2016 at thirteen years as the Big Time Batch."}],"traps":["There is no Booker's distillery: Jim Beam makes it, and Booker was a man, Booker Noe, the grandson of Jim Beam.","Each batch has its own proof: read it off the label in hand, and never quote one figure for the bourbon.","Booker's stands in Beam's Small Batch Bourbon Collection, but small batch has no legal meaning; uncut is its lesson."],"cards":[{"id":"uncut","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 bookers/uncut","prompt":"How is Booker's Bourbon bottled, by the producer's account?","answer":"Uncut and unfiltered: no water after the barrel and no chill filtering, so each batch has its own proof."},{"id":"gift-bottling","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 bookers/gift-bottling","prompt":"How did Booker Noe share his uncut bourbon before Booker's went on sale?","answer":"As holiday gifts to friends and family, bottled from barrels at the centre of the rackhouse."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 bookers/uncut","q":"A guest asks why each batch of Booker's Bourbon carries a different proof. What is the reason?","options":["Its recipe changes yearly","It is bottled uncut","It is finished in port","It is cut to a set proof"],"a":1,"explain":"No water goes in after the barrel, so each batch keeps the strength its barrels reached; the recipe itself stays the same from batch to batch."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 bookers/gift-bottling","q":"Before Booker's reached the shelves, how did Booker Noe share his barrel proof bourbon?","options":["Through Beam's 1992 collection","As an export bottling","As holiday gifts to friends","At a Louisville tasting bar"],"a":2,"explain":"He bottled it straight from the barrel as holiday gifts for friends and family; the Small Batch Bourbon Collection came in 1992, after Booker's was already on sale."}],"sources":[{"title":"Beam Distilling: About Booker's","url":"https://www.beamdistilling.com/bookersbourbon/about-bookers","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Booker's: the batches","url":"https://www.bookersbourbon.com/batches","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Beam Distilling: Booker's Rye, the Big Time Batch","url":"https://www.beamdistilling.com/bookersbourbon/whiskies/2016-le-limited-edition-bookers-rye-big-time-batch","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Booker's","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Booker%27s","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"VinePair: need to know, Booker's Bourbon","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/ntk-bookers-bourbon-guide/","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"george-t-stagg","group":"barrel-proof","family":"American","producer":"George T. Stagg","madeBy":"Buffalo Trace Distillery","style":"barrel-proof","chapter":"american-labels","shelf":"george-t-stagg","library":"","say":"","place":"Frankfort, Franklin County, Kentucky","year":"2002","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"George T. Stagg Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey, bottled uncut at barrel proof","mash":"Buffalo Trace's Mash Bill No. 1, by the distillery's own page: corn with rye and malted barley. No percentages are published.","still":"Distilled at the Buffalo Trace Distillery in Frankfort, on the Kentucky River; the distillery gives no details of the equipment for this bottling.","wood":"New charred oak, no less than fifteen years by the distillery's page, each barrel losing more than half its volume to evaporation. Each release states its own age.","strength":"Varies by release, uncut, the proof written on the label by hand","taste":"Toffee, dark chocolate, black cherry and tobacco on the nose, then a thick, hot palate of molasses, espresso and oak spice; water opens dried fruit and vanilla, and the finish is long and drying.","pour":"Neat with a jug of water on the side, or over one large cube. It is a bourbon for slow sipping, not for the mixing glass.","label":[{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Made in Kentucky as bourbon, aged at least two years in new charred oak, with nothing added."},{"term":"Proof","means":"Written on the front label by hand, since nobody knows it until the barrels are sampled and dumped."}]},"table":"George T. Stagg is the long aged barrel proof bourbon of Buffalo Trace in Frankfort, at least fifteen years in new charred oak, bottled uncut and unfiltered, its proof written on the label by hand. Expect toffee, black cherry and espresso, thick and hot, with water beside it.","story":"George T. Stagg (1835 to 1893) was a Kentuckian who sold whiskey in St. Louis and bought barrels of O.F.C. bourbon from Colonel E. H. Taylor Jr. When Taylor fell into debt, Stagg took over the O.F.C. distillery in Frankfort in 1878, moving from selling whiskey to making it. In 1904, eleven years after his death, the distillery took his name as the George T. Stagg Distillery. By the distillery's account it was one of six allowed to bottle whiskey for medicine through Prohibition, and Old Stagg became the bourbon it led with as Prohibition ended. The site was renamed Buffalo Trace in 1999, and its Antique Collection began in 2000. George T. Stagg joined the collection in 2002: barrels of the corn led Mash Bill No. 1, left fifteen years and more in new charred oak and bottled uncut and unfiltered in the autumn release. Over those summers each barrel gives more than half its volume to the air, and the strength left behind goes into the bottle, so the proof is written on by hand. For a bartender it is the long end of the barrel proof lesson: the same uncut idea as any batch, carried through fifteen years, and a bottle always poured with water beside it.","others":[{"name":"Stagg","note":"The younger, batched barrel proof bourbon from the same recipe, sold as Stagg Jr. until 2022."},{"name":"Old Stagg","note":"The distillery's flagship label at the close of Prohibition, revived uncut and unfiltered in Buffalo Trace's first Prohibition Collection."}],"traps":["George T. Stagg and Stagg are two bourbons: the same recipe, but Stagg has about half the years and comes in batches.","Its proof changes from release to release: read it off the label rather than quote a figure.","There is no separate Stagg distillery: it is made at Buffalo Trace, the site that carried Stagg's name from 1904 until 1999."],"cards":[{"id":"proof-by-hand","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 george-t-stagg/proof-by-hand","prompt":"How does the proof come to be on the front label of George T. Stagg?","answer":"It is written in by hand, because nobody knows it until the barrels are sampled and dumped."},{"id":"namesake","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 george-t-stagg/namesake","prompt":"Who was George T. Stagg, the man behind the bourbon's name?","answer":"A Kentucky-born whiskey salesman in St. Louis who took over E. H. Taylor's O.F.C. distillery in Frankfort."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 george-t-stagg/proof-by-hand","q":"A guest notices the proof on a bottle of George T. Stagg looks handwritten. How does that figure reach the label?","options":["Written in by hand","Printed months ahead","Stamped by the state","Carried over unchanged"],"a":0,"explain":"Each release's barrels are sampled and dumped before anyone knows the proof, so it is written in by hand; it is never printed ahead or carried over from another year."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 george-t-stagg/namesake","q":"A guest asks who George T. Stagg was, the man on the label. Which answer is right?","options":["A Frankfort mayor and distiller","A Bardstown grain farmer","A Louisville rackhouse builder","A St. Louis whiskey salesman"],"a":3,"explain":"Stagg sold whiskey in St. Louis and took over the O.F.C. distillery from E. H. Taylor Jr. around 1878; the Frankfort mayor and distiller who built O.F.C. was Taylor himself."}],"sources":[{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: George T. Stagg","url":"https://www.buffalotracedistillery.com/our-brands/george-t-stagg/george-t-stagg-bourbon/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: George T. Stagg, the bourbon's biography","url":"https://www.buffalotracedistillery.com/media-kit/bios/george-t-stagg.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: Old Stagg","url":"https://www.buffalotracedistillery.com/our-brands/prohibition-collection/old-stagg.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Whiskey University: George T. Stagg","url":"https://www.whiskeyuniv.com/george-t-stagg","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Distillery Trail: a Buffalo Trace timeline","url":"https://www.distillerytrail.com/blog/the-complete-buffalo-trace-distillery-timeline-1773-to-today/","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"stagg","group":"barrel-proof","family":"American","producer":"Stagg","madeBy":"Buffalo Trace Distillery","style":"barrel-proof","chapter":"american-labels","shelf":"stagg","library":"","say":"","place":"Frankfort, Franklin County, Kentucky","year":"2013","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"Stagg Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey, bottled uncut at barrel proof","mash":"Buffalo Trace's Mash Bill No. 1, the recipe of George T. Stagg: corn with rye and malted barley. No percentages are published.","still":"Distilled at the Buffalo Trace Distillery in Frankfort; the distillery gives no details of the equipment for this bottling.","wood":"New charred oak for about eight to nine years by the distillery's and the trade's accounts, roughly half the time of George T. Stagg. No age statement on the label.","strength":"Varies by batch, uncut, printed on each batch's label","taste":"Black cherry, caramel and brown sugar on the nose with a little oak char; a thick palate of dark chocolate, cinnamon and baked cherry; water brings out vanilla and dried fruit before a long, peppery finish.","pour":"Neat with water on the side, or over one large cube; a few drops of water bring the fruit forward.","label":[{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"A Kentucky bourbon of at least two years in new charred oak, its strength from the barrel."},{"term":"Batch","means":"Since 2022 a code of the year and a letter, as 22A, where Stagg Jr. carried running numbers."}]},"table":"Stagg, sold as Stagg Jr. until 2022, is the batched barrel proof bourbon of Buffalo Trace in Frankfort: the George T. Stagg recipe given about eight to nine years in oak, bottled uncut and unfiltered. Each batch has its own proof, with black cherry, caramel and dark chocolate.","story":"The name goes back to the distillery's past: by Buffalo Trace's account, Old Stagg was the bourbon the George T. Stagg Distillery led with as Prohibition ended, and the barrel proof bourbons that carry the name grew from it. George T. Stagg itself had been an autumn release of the Antique Collection since 2002, fifteen years and more in oak. In 2013 Buffalo Trace added a younger bourbon from the same Mash Bill No. 1 and called it Stagg Jr. Its opening batch was drawn from barrels of eight to nine years and bottled uncut and unfiltered, and the distillery meant to release several batches a year rather than one. The batches were numbered as they came, and the fifteenth was released in 2020. In 2022 the Jr. left the label and the bourbon became plain Stagg, its batches marked from then on by the year and a letter, as 22A. For a bartender the two bottles side by side teach what time does to barrel proof: one recipe, one uncut bottling, about half the years in wood for Stagg, and a proof to read off each batch's label rather than recall.","others":[{"name":"George T. Stagg","note":"The same recipe at fifteen years and more, an autumn release of the Antique Collection."},{"name":"Stagg Jr.","note":"This bourbon under its first name, on bottles of 2013 to 2021, in numbered batches."}],"traps":["A guest asking for Stagg Jr. wants this bourbon: the Jr. left the label in 2022 and the recipe did not change.","Stagg is not George T. Stagg: the same recipe and the same uncut bottling, with about half the years in oak.","Read the proof off the batch's label, since it changes from batch to batch and is always high."],"cards":[{"id":"age","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 stagg/age","prompt":"Roughly how long does Stagg rest in oak?","answer":"About eight to nine years, roughly half the fifteen or more of George T. Stagg; the label gives no age."},{"id":"stagg-jr","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 stagg/stagg-jr","prompt":"Under which name was Stagg sold before 2022?","answer":"Stagg Jr., first released in 2013; the Jr. left the label in 2022."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 stagg/age","q":"A guest asks how Stagg differs from George T. Stagg, made from the same recipe. What sets them apart?","options":["A wheated mash","A sherry cask finish","About half the years","A cut to 100 proof"],"a":2,"explain":"Both are Mash Bill No. 1 bottled uncut and unfiltered; Stagg simply has about eight to nine years in oak against fifteen and more, and neither is cut to 100 proof."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 stagg/stagg-jr","q":"A guest asks for a Stagg Jr. Which bottle on the shelf does the guest mean?","options":["Stagg, renamed in 2022","George T. Stagg, older","Old Stagg, a revival","Eagle Rare, ten years"],"a":0,"explain":"Stagg Jr. dropped the Jr. in 2022 and is sold as Stagg; George T. Stagg is the older bottling of the same recipe."}],"sources":[{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: Stagg","url":"https://www.buffalotracedistillery.com/our-brands/george-t-stagg/stagg-bourbon/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"The Spirits Business: Buffalo Trace releases Stagg Jr.","url":"https://www.thespiritsbusiness.com/2013/07/buffalo-trace-releases-stagg-jr-bourbon/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: the fifteenth Stagg Jr.","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/whiskey-styles/bourbon/new-stagg-jr-15th-release-bourbon-will-keep-you-warm-at-131-1-proof/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Daily Pour: Stagg Jr. sees a name change","url":"https://thedailypour.com/?p=20665","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Buffalo Trace Distillery: Old Stagg","url":"https://www.buffalotracedistillery.com/our-brands/prohibition-collection/old-stagg.html","kind":"primary"}]},
    {"key":"1792","group":"barrel-proof","family":"American","producer":"1792","madeBy":"Barton 1792 Distillery","style":"barrel-proof","chapter":"american-labels","shelf":"","library":"","say":"sev-en-teen NINE-tee-two","place":"Bardstown, Nelson County, Kentucky","year":"2002","yearIs":"first released","bottle":{"name":"1792 Full Proof Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","class":"Kentucky straight bourbon whiskey, bottled at its barrel entry proof","mash":"Corn, rye and malted barley, widely described as a high rye recipe; the distillery does not publish its mash bill.","still":"Distilled, aged and bottled at the Barton 1792 Distillery in Bardstown, by the producer's account; it gives no details of the equipment.","wood":"New charred oak barrels, filled at 125 proof. The label carries no age statement.","strength":"62.5% ABV (125 proof)","taste":"A deep, smoky char from the oak balanced by sweet vanilla and caramel, full and bold at this strength; a few drops of water soften the heat.","pour":"Neat with a little water, or over one large cube; at 125 proof it keeps its voice in an Old Fashioned or a Manhattan.","label":[{"term":"Full Proof","means":"Bottled at 125 proof, the strength at which the spirit went into the barrel."},{"term":"Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","means":"Bourbon distilled in Kentucky and aged at least two years in new charred oak, with nothing added."}]},"table":"1792 is the Bardstown bourbon of the Barton 1792 Distillery, named for the year Kentucky became a state. 1792 Full Proof is bottled at 125 proof, the strength at which it entered the barrel, close to but not quite barrel proof: vanilla, caramel and charred oak.","story":"The distillery behind the label stands at Bardstown, where a distillery was founded in 1879. In 2002 it brought out a small batch bourbon named for 1792, the year Kentucky became a state, at first as Ridgewood Reserve 1792. Brown-Forman, the maker of Woodford Reserve, sued over the name and the bottle, a federal judge ruled for it in 2004, and the bourbon became 1792 Ridgemont Reserve, later simply 1792. In 2009 the Sazerac Company bought the distillery and its brands from Constellation Brands, and the site took the name Barton 1792 Distillery. Full Proof followed in 2016. By the brand's account it is bottled at 125 proof, the strength at which its spirit went into new oak, and it skips chill filtering for a plate and frame filter; the launch release came from barrels filled in the autumn of 2007. Reviewers note that the bourbon leaves the barrel stronger and is brought back down to 125, so it is full proof, not barrel proof. For a bartender it is the plain lesson in the words on a strong label: barrel proof and cask strength mean bottled within two degrees of proof of the strength leaving the barrel, entry proof means the strength going in, and full proof is the house's own name for bottling at entry proof.","others":[{"name":"1792 Small Batch","note":"The house's standard bourbon, a marriage of chosen barrels at a gentler strength."},{"name":"1792 Bottled in Bond","note":"One distilling season, at least four years in a bonded warehouse, and exactly 100 proof."}],"traps":["Full proof is not barrel proof: by reviewers' accounts the bourbon leaves the barrel stronger and is brought back to its entry proof of 125.","1792 is not the year of the distillery: it marks Kentucky's statehood, and the bourbon was first sold in 2002.","The distillery is Barton 1792 in Bardstown; Buffalo Trace in Frankfort is a different distillery."],"cards":[{"id":"entry-proof","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 1792/entry-proof","prompt":"At what proof is 1792 Full Proof bottled, and what does that figure match?","answer":"125 proof, the strength at which the spirit went into the barrel."},{"id":"statehood","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 1792/statehood","prompt":"What does the number 1792 on the label of 1792 Full Proof mark?","answer":"The year Kentucky became a state; the bourbon was first sold in 2002."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 1792/entry-proof","q":"A guest asks if 1792 Full Proof is barrel proof. What does its 125 proof match?","options":["Its age in months","The bond law's minimum","Its distilling proof","Its barrel entry proof"],"a":3,"explain":"It is bottled at the strength at which the spirit was put into the barrel; the bond law asks exactly 100 proof, and the label gives no age at all."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 1792/statehood","q":"What does the number 1792 on a 1792 Full Proof label stand for?","options":["The distillery's founding","Kentucky's statehood","A barrel number","The bonded whiskey law"],"a":1,"explain":"It marks the year Kentucky became a state; the distillery at Bardstown dates from 1879."}],"sources":[{"title":"1792 Bourbon: Full Proof","url":"https://1792bourbon.com/full-proof/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"1792 Bourbon: defining moments","url":"https://1792bourbon.com/defining-moments/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: 1792 Bourbon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1792_Bourbon","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"VinePair: need to know, 1792 Bourbon","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/ntk-1792-bourbon-guide/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Modern Thirst: 1792 Full Proof","url":"https://modernthirst.com/2016/06/27/1792-full-proof-bourbon-review/","kind":"secondary"}]},
    {"key":"barrell","group":"barrel-proof","family":"American","producer":"Barrell","madeBy":"Barrell Craft Spirits","style":"barrel-proof","chapter":"american-labels","shelf":"","library":"","say":"","place":"Louisville, Jefferson County, Kentucky","year":"2013","yearIs":"founded","bottle":{"name":"Barrell Bourbon","class":"Straight bourbon whiskey, or, where a batch draws on several states, a blend of straight bourbon whiskeys","mash":"Differs by batch, a marriage of bourbons of several recipes; the house gives a mash bill derived for the whole on each batch's label.","still":"Not distilled by Barrell: it buys aged straight bourbon from distilleries in Tennessee, Kentucky and Indiana that it does not name, then blends and bottles it in Kentucky.","wood":"New charred oak, as straight bourbon requires. Ages differ by batch; from Batch 036 the label sets out the age of each whiskey in the blend.","strength":"Varies by batch, cask strength","taste":"Batches differ, and the house writes notes for each one: one batch runs from fruit punch and pineapple to aged wood, then oatmeal raisin cookie, sweet potato pie and crusty bread.","pour":"Neat, then with a few drops of water; over one large cube for a guest who finds it hot. A strong Old Fashioned takes it well.","label":[{"term":"Batch","means":"A number for each blend, as 038; every batch has its own sources, ages and proof."},{"term":"Cask Strength","means":"Bottled at the strength of the blend as it comes from the barrels, not cut down with water."}]},"table":"Barrell Bourbon is the Louisville blender's numbered series of straight bourbons bought from Tennessee, Kentucky and Indiana, married and bottled at cask strength without chill filtering. Each batch is its own bourbon, with its own ages and proof, so read the label before you describe it.","story":"Barrell Craft Spirits was founded in Louisville in 2013 by a founder who had made his career, by the house's account, in marketing and technology. It began as a blender and bottler, not a distiller: it bought aged straight bourbon, chose and married the barrels, and bottled the blend at the strength the barrels gave, without chill filtering, under a batch number. By September 2015 it had reached its fifth bourbon batch, and rye, blended whiskey and rum followed in the same way. The bourbon batches grew more involved as the numbers rose: whiskeys from Tennessee, Kentucky and Indiana in one batch or another, high rye and high corn recipes married together, and a mash bill derived for the whole. In 2024 Batch 036 brought a new label that sets out the states the whiskey came from and the age of each whiskey in the blend. For a bartender, Barrell Bourbon is barrel proof as a blender's choice rather than a distiller's: the strength is the barrels', the recipe is the blend, and the batch number is the bourbon, so a guest is told what this bottle holds and never what an earlier batch held.","others":[{"name":"Barrell Whiskey","note":"A separate series of blended whiskeys at cask strength, its batches often finished in other casks, from sherry and port to amaro."},{"name":"Barrell Seagrass","note":"A blend of American and Canadian ryes, each part finished in Martinique rum, apricot brandy or Madeira casks."}],"traps":["Barrell is a blender, not a distillery: it buys aged bourbon and marries it, and the name is spelt with two l's.","Each batch is a different blend: one batch's proof or tasting note does not describe the next."],"cards":[{"id":"blender","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 barrell/blender","prompt":"Does Barrell distil the bourbon in Barrell Bourbon?","answer":"No: it buys aged straight bourbon from distilleries in Tennessee, Kentucky and Indiana, then blends and bottles it."},{"id":"founded","key":"Cask Houses \u00b7 barrell/founded","prompt":"In which year was Barrell Craft Spirits founded, and where?","answer":"In 2013, in Louisville, Kentucky, as an independent blender and bottler."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","tag":"bottle","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 barrell/blender","q":"A guest asks which distillery makes Barrell Bourbon. What is the right answer?","options":["Bought bourbons, blended by Barrell","Barrell's stills in Louisville since 2013","Buffalo Trace, under contract","Jim Beam, at Clermont"],"a":0,"explain":"Barrell buys aged straight bourbon from several distilleries and blends it; it did not begin with stills of its own, and no single distillery makes it."},{"id":"q2","tag":"story","ckey":"Cask Houses \u00b7 barrell/founded","q":"In which year was Barrell Craft Spirits founded in Louisville?","options":["2009","2017","2013","2022"],"a":2,"explain":"The house dates its founding to 2013; Barrell Whiskey followed in 2015, and 2017 is too late."}],"sources":[{"title":"Barrell Craft Spirits: About us","url":"https://www.barrellbourbon.com/aboutus","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Barrell Craft Spirits: Barrell Bourbon","url":"https://www.barrellbourbon.com/bourbon","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Barrell Craft Spirits: earlier bourbon batches","url":"https://www.barrellbourbon.com/bourbonbatches","kind":"primary"},{"title":"BevNet: Barrell Bourbon Batch 036","url":"https://www.bevnet.com/pr/2024/05/29/barrell-craft-spirits-introduces-barrell-bourbon-batch-036","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Breaking Bourbon: Barrell Whiskey Batch 001","url":"https://www.breakingbourbon.com/review/barrell-whiskey-batch-001","kind":"secondary"}]}
